window.S=[{"n":1,"tr":"Ar-Rahman","ar":"الرحمن","m":"The Most Merciful","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ar-Rahman comes from *rahma*, mercy. It describes mercy so wide that it reaches everyone and everything: believer and unbeliever, human and animal, in this life. Nobody earns it. It simply flows from God, like sunlight that falls on a whole city without asking who deserves it."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"Every chapter but one begins with this name.\n> “In the name of God, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate.” (1:1)\n> “My mercy encompasses all things.” (7:156)\nThe 55th chapter is named after it: Ar-Rahman."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said God created mercy in one hundred parts. He sent one part down to earth, and that single part is why a mother is gentle with her child and why even an animal lifts its hoof so as not to hurt its young. God kept the other ninety-nine for the Day of Judgment. (Bukhari, Muslim)\nOnce a woman was brought among the captives, frantically searching for her baby. When she found him she held him to her chest and fed him. The Prophet ﷺ asked his companions, “Would this woman throw her child into a fire?” They said, “Never.” He said, “God is more merciful to His servants than this woman is to her child.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of the strongest love you have seen between a parent and a child. Now remember what the Prophet ﷺ said: all of that is just one part out of a hundred."},{"h":"Live it","t":"He ﷺ said: “The merciful are shown mercy by the Most Merciful. Be merciful to those on earth, and the One above the heavens will be merciful to you.” (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi) Start small: be gentle with someone today who has not earned it."}]},{"n":2,"tr":"Ar-Rahim","ar":"الرحيم","m":"The Especially Merciful","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ar-Rahim is the partner of Ar-Rahman. Scholars often explain it this way: Ar-Rahman is mercy for everyone, Ar-Rahim is mercy that takes special care of those who turn to Him, above all in the next life. Ar-Rahman is the rain on the whole field; Ar-Rahim is the gardener who tends your plant."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “And He is ever Merciful to the believers.” (33:43)\n> “Your Lord has made mercy incumbent upon Himself.” (6:54)\nIt sits right beside Ar-Rahman in the opening line of the Quran, so the first thing we learn about God is mercy, twice."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"The Prophet ﷺ once kissed his grandson Hasan while al-Aqra’ ibn Habis was sitting with him. Al-Aqra’ said, “I have ten children and I have never kissed any of them.” The Prophet ﷺ looked at him and said, “Whoever shows no mercy will be shown no mercy.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A doctor treats every patient who walks in. But the one who sits with you at night, learns your name and follows up on your recovery shows a closer care. That is the feeling of this name."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Mercy you show is mercy you will receive. Forgive someone, be patient with a child, or give quietly to someone in need, and let it be done as a kind of thanks."}]},{"n":3,"tr":"Al-Malik","ar":"الملك","m":"The King","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Malik is the King, the one who truly owns, rules and commands. Human kings borrow their power, run out of it, and need helpers. God's kingship is complete. Everything that exists belongs to Him and does only what He allows."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is God, other than whom there is no god: the Sovereign, the Pure, the Source of Peace…” (59:23)\n> “Say: O God, Owner of all sovereignty. You give sovereignty to whom You will and take it from whom You will.” (3:26)\n> “The King of mankind.” (114:2)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"On the Day of Judgment God will fold up the heavens and the earth and say, “I am the King. Where are the kings of the earth?” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nHe ﷺ also said that the most shameful name before God on that Day belongs to the man who called himself “King of Kings.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Every empire in history, Rome, Persia, the Mongols, felt permanent to the people living in it. All of them are gone. The King of everything has not changed."},{"h":"Live it","t":"If He is the King, you do not have to fear anyone's power or flatter anyone for provision. Be humble with people: only One is entitled to the title."}]},{"n":4,"tr":"Al-Quddus","ar":"القدوس","m":"The Most Pure","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Quddus means the One who is absolutely pure and holy, free of every flaw, weakness or resemblance to His creation. Nothing about God needs fixing, and nothing can stain Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “…the Sovereign, the Pure, the Source of Peace…” (59:23)\n> “Whatever is in the heavens and on earth glorifies God, the Sovereign, the Pure, the Almighty, the Wise.” (62:1)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In bowing and in prostration in prayer, he ﷺ used to say: “Subbuhun, Quddus, Rabbul-mala’ikati war-ruh” — “Perfect and Pure, Lord of the angels and the Spirit.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A mirror that is perfectly clean has nothing to hide and nothing to add. Now imagine a purity with no limit at all. Even our best praise falls short of it."},{"h":"Live it","t":"You borrow a share of this name by keeping what you can clean: your body, your heart, your tongue. Ask yourself what in you needs cleaning up today, and start with one thing."}]},{"n":5,"tr":"As-Salam","ar":"السلام","m":"The Source of Peace","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"As-Salam is the source of all peace and safety. Peace is what He is, and peace is what He gives, in a heart, a home, a nation, and finally in Paradise, which the Quran calls the Home of Peace."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “…the Sovereign, the Pure, the Source of Peace…” (59:23)\n> “God invites to the Home of Peace and guides whom He wills to a straight path.” (10:25)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"After finishing the prayer he ﷺ would ask forgiveness three times, then say: “O God, You are Peace and from You comes peace. Blessed are You, Lord of Majesty and Generosity.” (Muslim)\nHe ﷺ also said: “You will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another. Shall I tell you something that will make you love one another? Spread the greeting of peace among yourselves.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of the moment after a long argument ends, or of coming home after a long journey. That relief in the chest is a small taste of what this name describes."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Every time you say “as-salamu alaykum” you are saying His name over someone. Mean it: greet first, and leave people feeling safer than before you arrived."}]},{"n":6,"tr":"Al-Mu'min","ar":"المؤمن","m":"The Giver of Security","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mu’min is the One who gives security and who is the source of safety. He removes fear from the hearts of those who turn to Him, and He never breaks His promise to anyone. It shares a root with *iman*, faith, and with *amn*, safety: trust and safety are family."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “…the Source of Peace, the Granter of Security, the Guardian…” (59:23)\n> “[He is] the One who fed them against hunger and secured them from fear.” (106:4)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr hid in the cave during the migration to Medina, their pursuers stood so close that Abu Bakr whispered that if one of them looked down, he would see them. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not grieve; God is with us.” (9:40) When Abraham was thrown into the fire, God said, “O fire, be coolness and safety for Abraham.” (21:69)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"“The believer is the one from whom people's lives and property are safe.” (Tirmidhi, Nasa’i)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be someone others feel safe around: do not spread rumors, break trust, or make people afraid of your temper. And when fear grips you, turn to the One who gives security."}]},{"n":7,"tr":"Al-Muhaymin","ar":"المهيمن","m":"The Guardian","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muhaymin is the Guardian who watches over everything, knows its condition, and protects it. Nothing runs without Him, and nothing slips out of His sight. The word carries the feeling of a protector who never takes his eyes off what he guards."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “…the Granter of Security, the Guardian, the Almighty, the Compeller…” (59:23)\nThe Quran is also described as *muhayminan*, a guardian over the earlier scriptures (5:48)."},{"h":"Story","t":"Moses' mother feared for her baby son when Pharaoh was killing newborn boys. God told her to place him in the river and not to fear or grieve, because He would return him to her. (28:7) The river carried him straight into Pharaoh's own household, and he was raised there under the very roof of the man who hunted him. She put her child in the water and God held him."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A lifeguard sees the whole beach at once, not only the swimmer in trouble. Now take away every limit of attention and fatigue."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When you hand something over to God, a worry, a child, a decision, you are leaving it with a Guardian who does not sleep. Say it plainly in your own words, and then leave it there."}]},{"n":8,"tr":"Al-Aziz","ar":"العزيز","m":"The Almighty","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Aziz means the Almighty: the One whose power cannot be overcome and whose honor cannot be taken away. It mixes strength with dignity. Nobody can force Him, defeat Him or belittle Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Whoever desires honor, then to God belongs all honor.” (35:10)\n> “Victory comes only from God, the Almighty, the Wise.” (3:126)\nThat verse is about the Battle of Badr, where about 300 poorly equipped believers faced a force roughly three times their number and won."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A small person may be treated with dignity because of who stands behind them. The believers at Badr had nothing but God behind them, and that proved enough."},{"h":"Live it","t":"People chase honor through money, titles and followers, and it can all disappear overnight. Real dignity comes from obeying the One who holds it. Do the right thing even when it costs you status and trust that your honor is safe with Him."}]},{"n":9,"tr":"Al-Jabbar","ar":"الجبار","m":"The Restorer and Compeller","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Jabbar has two beautiful meanings. First, the One who sets right what is broken, from the Arabic *jabr*, setting a bone. Second, the One whose will can never be resisted. For a person in pain, the first meaning is the sweetest."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “…the Almighty, the Compeller, the Supremely Great.” (59:23)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"Between the two prostrations in prayer he ﷺ used to say: “O God, forgive me, have mercy on me, mend me (wajburni), guide me and provide for me.” (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi) Every prayer includes a moment where we ask Al-Jabbar to put us back together."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A broken bone, once set, can become stronger at the point of the break. That is what this name does for a broken heart, a failed plan or a lost year."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Make this dua in your own words when you feel broken. Be a means of mending for others: a call to someone grieving or a word to someone who failed. People are not meant to be a compeller over one another, so do not use this name's other meaning to dominate anyone."}]},{"n":10,"tr":"Al-Mutakabbir","ar":"المتكبر","m":"The Supreme in Greatness","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mutakabbir means the One who is supreme in greatness. Pride is a flaw in us, because we have nothing to be proud of that we did not receive. Pride and greatness truly belong to God alone, because He alone is truly great."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “…the Almighty, the Compeller, the Supremely Great. Glory be to God above what they associate with Him.” (59:23)\nThe first act of pride in the Quran is Iblis refusing to bow to Adam: “I am better than him. You created me from fire and him from clay.” (7:12)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God says: “Honor is My lower garment and pride is My cloak. Whoever competes with Me over either of them, I will punish him.” (Muslim)\nHe ﷺ also said no one with even an atom's weight of pride in his heart will enter Paradise. A man asked about someone who likes good clothes and shoes. He ﷺ replied that God is beautiful and loves beauty; pride is rejecting the truth and looking down on people. (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"The tallest person in a crowd is still small next to a mountain. Pride is a person standing on a pebble and calling it a mountain."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Watch for the small forms: needing the last word, looking down on someone, refusing to say sorry. Each time, say “Allahu Akbar,” meaning that God is greater than my ego."}]},{"n":11,"tr":"Al-Khaliq","ar":"الخالق","m":"The Creator","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Khaliq is the Creator. The Arabic word also carries the idea of planning and measuring before making, like an architect who draws the full design before the first stone is laid. God plans every creature, then brings it into being from nothing.\nNote: Al-Khaliq, Al-Bari and Al-Musawwir appear together in one verse. Al-Ghazali explains them as three stages: planning, making and shaping."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is God, the Creator, the Maker, the Fashioner. His are the most beautiful names.” (59:24)\n> “Say: God is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the Prevailing.” (13:16)\nThe very first word revealed to the Prophet ﷺ pointed here: “Read, in the name of your Lord who created.” (96:1)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Quran walks through how a human being is made: from a drop, to a clinging clot, to a lump, to bones that are then clothed with flesh, “and then We produced it as another creation. So blessed is God, the best of creators.” (23:12–14) Every person you have ever met went through this journey, hidden in the dark, in the care of the Creator."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A painter can only rearrange colors that already exist. God alone gives existence itself. Nothing He made was copied from anything."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Look at what He made and let it move you: a tree, the rain, your own hand. The Quran praises people who “reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth” and say, “Our Lord, You did not create this without purpose.” (3:191) Ten quiet minutes outside with that thought counts as worship."}]},{"n":12,"tr":"Al-Bari","ar":"البارئ","m":"The Maker","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Bari is the Maker: the One who carries out the plan and brings each creature into being, free of any flaw. The word also suggests setting things apart, so every creature is its own distinct kind. If Al-Khaliq draws the design, Al-Bari builds it.\nNote: the Quran gives this name in 59:24, and also when Moses tells his people to turn back to “your Maker” (2:54)."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is God, the Creator, the Maker, the Fashioner.” (59:24)\n> “Turn in repentance to your Maker.” (2:54)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God has prescribed excellence (*ihsan*) in everything.” (Muslim) The Maker makes everything with perfect craft, and He asks the same care from us in what we do."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A designer, a builder and a painter can all work on one house. Al-Bari is the One who actually makes it stand, and everything in it fits because He made each piece for its place."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Take pride in doing your work well, even when no one is checking: the report, the meal, the prayer. Do it as someone who knows the Maker loves a job done properly."}]},{"n":13,"tr":"Al-Musawwir","ar":"المصور","m":"The Fashioner","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Musawwir is the Fashioner, the One who gives every creature its own shape and face. The Creator planned it, the Maker made it, and the Fashioner gave it its final form. That is why a rose is not a lily, and why no two faces in the world are exactly alike."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “It is He who shapes you in the wombs however He wills.” (3:6)\n> “He shaped you and made your forms good.” (40:64)\n> “O mankind, what has deceived you about your Lord, the Generous, who created you, proportioned you and balanced you? In whatever form He willed, He assembled you.” (82:6–8)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God does not look at your bodies or your appearance, but He looks at your hearts and your deeds.” (Muslim)\nHe also taught this dua: “O God, as You made my form good, make my character good.” (Ahmad)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Look at the faces on a train. Billions of people have lived, and no two have had the same face. That variety is the work of the Fashioner."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Don’t look down on anyone for how they look: nobody chose their own form. Make the Prophet’s ﷺ dua when you look in the mirror, and work on the one thing that is yours to shape, your character."}]},{"n":14,"tr":"Al-Ghaffar","ar":"الغفار","m":"The Constant Forgiver","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Ghaffar is the One who forgives again and again. The word comes from *ghafara*, to cover. The shape of the name means repeated and constant: not once, but every time you come back. Your sins are covered so completely that you can start again."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “I am forgiving to whoever repents, believes, does good, and then stays on the path.” (20:82)\n> “Seek forgiveness from your Lord. Indeed He is ever Forgiving.” (71:10, Noah to his people)\n> “O My servants who have wronged themselves, do not despair of God’s mercy. God forgives all sins.” (39:53)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God says: “O son of Adam, as long as you call on Me and hope in Me, I forgive you whatever you have done, and I do not mind. O son of Adam, if your sins reached the clouds of the sky and then you asked My forgiveness, I would forgive you. O son of Adam, if you came to Me with sins nearly as large as the earth, and then met Me without associating anything with Me, I would come to you with forgiveness nearly as large as the earth.” (Tirmidhi)\nThe Prophet ﷺ himself, whose sins were already forgiven, said: “O people, turn to God in repentance, for I turn to Him a hundred times a day.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of a friend who is happy to take your call at three in the morning, again and again. The Forgiver is happier to hear your “I’m sorry” than you are to say it."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Say *astaghfirullah* often, especially after small slips. And take a share of this name by covering the faults of others. The Prophet ﷺ said that whoever hides the fault of a Muslim, God will hide his faults on the Day of Judgment. (Bukhari, Muslim)"}]},{"n":15,"tr":"Al-Qahhar","ar":"القهار","m":"The Subduer","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Qahhar is the Subduer: the One whose power overwhelms everything. Every ruler, every empire and every force in nature moves only by His permission. Nothing can stand against Him, and nothing can escape Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Say: God is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the Subduer.” (13:16)\n> “To whom belongs the kingdom today? To God, the One, the Subduer!” (40:16, said on the Day of Judgment, with no one else to answer)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Pharaoh said, “I am your lord, the most high.” (79:24) He had armies, a palace and a nation that obeyed him. In the end he was drowned in the sea he thought he controlled, and only then did he say, “I believe.” (10:90) The One who subdues had the last word."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “The strong person is not the one who overcomes others by force. The strong person is the one who controls himself when angry.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"This name does two things. Fear of tyrants shrinks, because they too are under His control. And you are invited to subdue what overpowers you from within, your anger and your cravings, so that they do not rule you."}]},{"n":16,"tr":"Al-Wahhab","ar":"الوهاب","m":"The Bestower","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wahhab is the Bestower: the One who gives gifts continuously, without being asked, without needing anything back, and without running out. A gift is something given freely, with no payment. Everything good you have is that kind of gift."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Our Lord, do not let our hearts swerve after You have guided us, and grant us from Yourself mercy. Indeed You are the Bestower.” (3:8)\nThe same root is in the prayer of Zakariyya (Zechariah): “My Lord, grant me (*hab li*) from Yourself a good offspring.” (3:38)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Zakariyya was very old and his wife was unable to have children. Yet he called quietly on his Lord for an heir, and God answered with the news of a son, Yahya (John). (19:2–7) Solomon asked for a kingdom that no one after him would have, and began his prayer by saying “You are the Bestower” (38:35). God gave it to him."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Imagine a gift you never earned, never asked for and could never repay. That describes your eyesight, your next breath, and the people who love you."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Say the dua of 3:8 often, especially for your heart to stay firm. And practice this name by giving something with no strings attached and no expectation of thanks."}]},{"n":17,"tr":"Ar-Razzaq","ar":"الرزاق","m":"The Provider","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ar-Razzaq is the Provider: the One who supplies everyone and everything with what it needs, every day. The shape of the name means constant and abundant. He has not stopped providing since the first creature."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed it is God who is the Provider, the Possessor of firm strength.” (51:58)\n> “There is no creature on earth but its provision is upon God.” (11:6)\n> “How many a creature does not carry its own provision. God provides for it and for you.” (29:60)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Mary (Maryam) lived in the prayer chamber, and whenever Zakariyya came in he found food with her. He asked, “O Mary, where does this come from?” She said, “It is from God. Indeed God provides for whom He wills without account.” (3:37) Seeing that made Zakariyya bold enough to pray for a child of his own."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “No soul will die until it has received its full provision. So fear God and be moderate in seeking it: take what is lawful and leave what is unlawful.” (Ibn Majah)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Work, because effort is part of how provision arrives. But hold your worry loosely, and keep your earnings clean. When you eat, remember who fed you."}]},{"n":18,"tr":"Al-Fattah","ar":"الفتاح","m":"The Opener","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Fattah is the Opener: the One who opens closed doors. He opens the door of provision, of mercy, of knowledge, of ease, of a locked heart, and of victory. The same word also means the Judge, because a verdict “opens” a dispute that was stuck."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Say: Our Lord will bring us together, then judge between us with truth. And He is the Opener, the All-Knowing.” (34:26)\n> “Whatever mercy God opens for people, none can hold it back.” (35:2)"},{"h":"Story","t":"At Hudaybiyyah the Muslims were stopped from entering Makkah, and the treaty looked like a loss. Some of the companions were very upset. Then God revealed, “Indeed We have granted you a clear opening (*fath*).” (48:1) Two years later Makkah was opened to the Muslims without a battle. A closed door can be a door that has not opened yet."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"When entering the mosque he ﷺ would say: “O God, open for me the doors of Your mercy.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"When you are stuck, with a job, a problem, a hard heart, don’t only push harder. Ask the Opener, and keep doing your part. Try the mosque dua and watch for which door opens."}]},{"n":19,"tr":"Al-Alim","ar":"العليم","m":"The All-Knowing","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Alim is the All-Knowing: His knowledge has no beginning and no end, and nothing is hidden from it. He knows what happened, what is happening and what will happen. He knows what you feel but cannot put into words."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “With Him are the keys of the unseen; none knows them but Him. He knows what is on land and in the sea. Not a leaf falls without His knowing it.” (6:59)\n> “Does He who created not know? And He is the Subtle, the All-Aware.” (67:14)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"When the angel Gabriel, in the form of a man, asked him about the Day of Judgment, the Prophet ﷺ replied, “The one being asked knows no more than the one asking.” (Muslim) Even the greatest of creation said this knowledge belongs to God alone.\nHe also taught the prayer for guidance (*istikhara*): “O God, I ask You to choose what is good, by Your knowledge, and I ask You for ability by Your power. You have power and I have none. You know and I do not know. You are the Knower of the unseen.” (Bukhari)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A map shows a few roads. Al-Alim is like someone who knows every road, every house and every person, in every time, all at once."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Don’t worry that God does not know your situation. Say the *istikhara* dua before big decisions. And grow in humility and learning: “My Lord, increase me in knowledge.” (20:114)"}]},{"n":20,"tr":"Al-Qabid","ar":"القابض","m":"The Withholder","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Qabid is the One who withholds, holds back and gathers. Beside Al-Basit, the One who expands, it shows that God tightens and widens: provision, health, comfort and even the feelings in the heart. The two names are understood together.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “He withholds and He expands.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Who will lend God a good loan, which He will multiply for him many times over? It is God who withholds and expands, and to Him you will be returned.” (2:245)\n> “God extends provision for whom He wills and restricts it.” (13:26)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"When prices rose in Madinah, people asked him to fix prices. He said: “God is the One who sets prices, the Withholder, the Expander, the Provider. I hope to meet my Lord with none of you claiming I wronged him in blood or in wealth.” (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Years of pressure closed in on the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah. He went to Ta’if hoping for support and was turned away. He later described that day as the hardest he had faced. (Bukhari, Muslim) Around the same period came the Night Journey and the Ascension, one of the greatest honors any human has received. After the squeeze, the expansion."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When a season of life feels tight, remember that tightness is not forever and is not random. Be patient, keep praying, and give from whatever you have, because the Withholder is also the One who repays."}]},{"n":21,"tr":"Al-Basit","ar":"الباسط","m":"The Expander","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Basit is the Expander: the One who spreads out, widens and opens up. He expands provision for some, space for hearts, time for work, and relief after a squeeze. It is the other half of Al-Qabid, and together they describe how life really moves: tight, then wide, then tight again, all in His hands.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “He extends provision for whom He wills.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God extends provision for whom He wills and restricts it.” (13:26)\n> “Indeed your Lord extends provision for whom He wills and restricts it. Indeed He is ever All-Aware and All-Seeing of His servants.” (17:30)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Whoever would like his provision to be expanded and his life to be extended, let him keep good ties with his family.” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nHe also taught that God “stretches out His hand at night so that the one who sinned by day may repent, and stretches out His hand by day so that the one who sinned by night may repent.” (Muslim) The door of return is never closed."},{"h":"Story","t":"During a heavy time in the Prophet’s ﷺ life, God reminded him: “Did We not expand your chest for you?” and then, twice, “Indeed, with hardship comes ease.” (94:1, 5–6)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be wide-hearted. Forgive widely, host widely, give widely. Whoever expands for others finds the Expander expanding for them."}]},{"n":22,"tr":"Al-Khafid","ar":"الخافض","m":"The Abaser","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Khafid is the One who lowers and brings down. When people puff themselves up with wealth, status or power, and refuse the truth, He can bring them low. It is not random. It is the fair result of climbing over others and over God’s limits.\nNote: the Quran says the Day of Judgment will be one that “brings down some and raises up others” (56:3). The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “It will bring down some and raise up others.” (56:3)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Qarun (Korah) was so rich that the keys to his treasures were a burden for a group of strong men. His people warned him not to boast. He answered, “I was given this because of knowledge I have.” (28:76–78) Those who wanted worldly life said, “If only we had what Qarun was given!” Then God made the earth swallow him and his house, and he had no one to help him. (28:79–81) By the end, the people who had envied him were saying how fortunate they were not to have been in his place. (28:82)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God raises some people by this Book (the Quran) and lowers others by it.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Check what you are leaning on. If it is your money, your rank or your cleverness, those are exactly the things that can be taken. Stay humble, and hold on to what lifts you for good: knowledge, honesty and prayer."}]},{"n":23,"tr":"Ar-Rafi","ar":"الرافع","m":"The Exalter","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ar-Rafi is the One who raises and lifts up: ranks in this life and the next, the humble, the people of faith and knowledge. Where Al-Khafid brings down pride, Ar-Rafi lifts those who stay low before Him.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “We raise in degrees whom We will.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God will raise those of you who believe, and those who have been given knowledge, by degrees.” (58:11)\n> “We raise in degrees whom We will.” (6:83)\n> “And We raised high for you your remembrance.” (94:4)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Think of the Prophet ﷺ: an orphan, poor in his early life. God told him, “We raised high your remembrance.” Today, five times a day, from every minaret in the world, his name is called out beside God’s name. No king could have arranged that."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Charity never decreases wealth. No one forgives but God increases his honor. And no one humbles himself for God but God raises him.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Want to rise? Go low. Serve, learn, forgive. The Prophet ﷺ promised that the one who humbles himself for God is lifted by God."}]},{"n":24,"tr":"Al-Mu'izz","ar":"المعز","m":"The Giver of Honor","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mu’izz is the One who gives honor and strength. People chase honor through money, titles and followers. Real honor, *izza*, is a gift from God. He gives it to whom He wills, and nobody can take it from them.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “You honor whom You will.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “You honor whom You will, and You humble whom You will. In Your hand is all good.” (3:26)\n> “Honor belongs to God, to His Messenger and to the believers.” (63:8)\n> “Do they seek honor from them? Indeed, all honor belongs to God.” (4:139)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Yusuf (Joseph) was thrown into a well by his own brothers, sold for a few coins, slandered, and kept in prison for years. Then the king said to him, “Today you are established with us and trusted.” Yusuf asked to be put in charge of the land’s storehouses, and the Quran says, “Thus We established Yusuf in the land.” (12:54–56) The honor came from God, not from the people who had tried to bury him."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A person with a famous name may be treated well, but only as long as the name holds. Honor given by God is not tied to anyone’s opinion."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Never trade your principles to gain approval. People who sell themselves cheaply to win honor usually lose both. Hold to what is right, and leave your honor with the One who grants it."}]},{"n":25,"tr":"Al-Mudhill","ar":"المذل","m":"The Humbler","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mudhill is the One who humbles. He brings low those who are arrogant and keep refusing the truth. It is always understood beside Al-Mu’izz, the Giver of honor: He is just in both.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “You humble whom You will” (3:26). The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “You honor whom You will, and You humble whom You will.” (3:26)\nThere is also a good kind of lowliness: “Lower to your parents the wing of humility out of mercy.” (17:24)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When Iblis refused to bow to Adam because he thought himself better, God said, “Get down from it. It is not for you to be arrogant in it. So get out. Indeed you are of the humbled ones.” (7:13) The one who thought he was too high for a single act of respect lost his place."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Pride is a ladder with no top. Whoever climbs it for the sake of ego eventually has nowhere to stand."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be the kind of humble that God loves: soft toward your parents, gentle with the weak, sincere in your sujud. If you are not willing to lower yourself in front of God, you are choosing the other way of being lowered."}]},{"n":26,"tr":"As-Sami","ar":"السميع","m":"The All-Hearing","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"As-Sami is the All-Hearing: He hears every sound, whisper and unspoken wish, at once, in every language, without confusion. Nothing is too soft, too far or too many for Him. He does not just hear, He responds."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Our Lord, accept this from us. You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.” (2:127, Ibrahim and Ismail building the Ka’ba)\n> “God has heard the speech of the woman who disputes with you about her husband and complains to God. God hears your conversation.” (58:1)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A woman named Khawla came to the Prophet ﷺ in distress about how her husband had treated her. Aisha, who was in the same room, said that she could not make out all of what Khawla was saying. Aisha’s words were: “Glory to the One whose hearing encompasses all voices. I was in the corner of the house, and some of her words were hidden from me.” Then God revealed, “God has heard the speech of the one who disputes with you.” (Ibn Majah) A private complaint, in a corner of a house, became part of the Quran forever."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"When the companions were raising their voices in remembrance on a journey, he said: “O people, be gentle with yourselves. You are not calling on one who is deaf or absent. You are calling on One who is All-Hearing and Near.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Your whispered dua is heard. Speak to God as you would to someone sitting right beside you. And be a good listener yourself: give people your full attention when they speak."}]},{"n":27,"tr":"Al-Basir","ar":"البصير","m":"The All-Seeing","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Basir is the All-Seeing: He sees everything, big and small, visible or hidden, in light or darkness, without eyes or any limit. He sees your work, your struggle and the good you do when nobody notices."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Glory to Him who took His servant by night… Indeed He is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.” (17:1)\n> “[He] sees you when you stand [in prayer], and your movements among those who prostrate.” (26:218–219)\n> “Do not fear. I am with you both, hearing and seeing.” (20:46, God to Moses and Aaron before going to Pharaoh)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"When Gabriel asked him what *ihsan* (excellence) is, he said: “It is to worship God as though you see Him. And if you do not see Him, He certainly sees you.” (Muslim)\nHe also listed seven kinds of people who will be in God’s shade on the Day of Judgment, among them “a man who remembered God in private and his eyes overflowed with tears.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of how differently you act when someone you respect is watching. Now picture being watched with love by the One who knows your whole story."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Let this name do two things. It guards you when no one is looking, and it comforts you when no one notices your effort. The tired parent, the unnoticed helper, the one who prays in the dark: all of it is seen."}]},{"n":28,"tr":"Al-Hakam","ar":"الحكم","m":"The Judge","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Hakam is the Judge: the One who decides between people with perfect fairness, and whose decision is final. Every dispute that is unresolved in this world will be settled by Him. This is a great comfort to anyone who has been wronged."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Shall I seek a judge other than God, when it is He who has sent down to you the Book explained in detail?” (6:114)\n> “Judgment belongs to God, the Most High, the Most Great.” (40:12)\n> “Is not God the most just of judges?” (95:8)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A man known as Abu al-Hakam (“father of the judge”) came to the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said, “God is Al-Hakam, and judgment belongs to Him. Why are you called Abu al-Hakam?” The man said, “When my people disagree, they come to me and I judge between them, and both sides are satisfied.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “How good that is! Do you have children?” He had three: Shurayh, Muslim and Abdullah. “Who is the eldest?” “Shurayh.” “Then you are Abu Shurayh.” (Abu Dawud, Nasa’i) The Prophet ﷺ gently moved him away from a title that belongs to God alone."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “I am only a human being, and you bring your disputes to me. Perhaps one of you argues his case better than another, and I judge for him according to what I hear. Whoever I give something of his brother’s right to, it is only a piece of Fire that I am giving him.” (Bukhari, Muslim) Even the Prophet’s ﷺ ruling cannot make a wrong into a right. Only the true Judge sees everything."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Let go of the need to win every argument or to settle every score. If you have been wronged and can’t prove it, the Judge knows. If you judge between others, do it as someone who will answer for it."}]},{"n":29,"tr":"Al-Adl","ar":"العدل","m":"The Just","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Adl is the Just: He puts every right in its place and wrongs nobody, not by even an atom’s weight. Every wrong done in this life will be set right, every good will be weighed.\nNote: the Quran speaks of God’s justice again and again, though “Al-Adl” as a title is part of the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed God does not wrong [anyone] even by an atom’s weight.” (4:40)\n> “We will set up the scales of justice on the Day of Resurrection, so no soul will be wronged in the least.” (21:47)\n> “Indeed God commands justice and doing good.” (16:90)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God says: “O My servants, I have forbidden injustice for Myself and have made it forbidden among you, so do not wrong one another.” (Muslim)\nHe also said that on the Day of Judgment rights will be returned to those they are owed, “even the hornless sheep will be given justice against the horned sheep.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A noble woman of the Makhzum clan was caught stealing. Her family were afraid of the punishment, so they sent Usama, whom the Prophet ﷺ loved dearly, to plead for her. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Do you intercede concerning one of God’s limits?” Then he stood and said, “Those before you were destroyed because when a noble among them stole, they let him go, and when a weak person stole, they punished him. By God, if Fatima, daughter of Muhammad, were to steal, I would cut off her hand.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be fair even when it costs you: with family, at work, with people you dislike. “Do not let hatred of a people lead you to be unjust. Be just; it is closer to God-consciousness.” (5:8)"}]},{"n":30,"tr":"Al-Latif","ar":"اللطيف","m":"The Subtle, the Gentle","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Latif is the Subtle and Gentle: the One who knows the finest details of everything and who brings about good in quiet, unseen ways. His help often arrives so gently and from such an unexpected direction that you only understand it later."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed my Lord is Subtle in what He wills.” (12:100, Yusuf)\n> “Eyes cannot perceive Him, but He perceives all eyes. He is the Subtle, the All-Aware.” (6:103)\n> “Even if something weighs as little as a mustard seed, and is hidden inside a rock or anywhere in the heavens or the earth, God will bring it forth. Indeed God is Subtle, All-Aware.” (31:16)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Looking back at his life, Yusuf could see how every hard step, the well, the slavery, the prison, was a quiet path toward the dream he had as a boy. At the end he said, “Indeed my Lord is Subtle in what He wills.” (12:100) At the time, none of it looked like kindness."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God is gentle and loves gentleness. He gives for gentleness what He does not give for harshness, and what He does not give for anything else.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"When life takes a turn you did not expect, say “Latif.” Trust that hidden kindnesses are in motion. And bring your own quiet gentleness to people: a soft word does what a hard one cannot."}]},{"n":31,"tr":"Al-Khabir","ar":"الخبير","m":"The All-Aware","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Khabir is the All-Aware: the One who knows the inside of things, their hidden details and how they really work. Al-Alim knows everything. Al-Khabir adds that His knowledge reaches the inner reality, down to the intention sitting in a heart."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is the Subduer over His servants, and He is the Wise, the All-Aware.” (6:18)\n> “Fear God. Indeed God is All-Aware of what you do.” (59:18)\n> “Indeed God is All-Knowing, All-Aware.” (49:13)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Prophet ﷺ once confided something to one of his wives, and she told it to another. God let him know about it. When he mentioned part of it to her, she asked, “Who told you this?” He said, “I was informed by the All-Knowing, the All-Aware.” (66:3) She had thought no one knew."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Actions are judged by intentions, and everyone will get what they intended.” (Bukhari, Muslim) Al-Khabir knows what is behind every deed."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Two people may hand the same gift to the same person. One does it to help and the other to show off. Only the All-Aware tells the difference."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Clean up the inside. Check why you are doing the good you do. And take comfort that every effort made for Him, however small or hidden, is known to Him exactly as it is."}]},{"n":32,"tr":"Al-Halim","ar":"الحليم","m":"The Forbearing","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Halim is the Forbearing: the One who sees everything people do wrong and still does not rush to punish. He waits and gives time to turn back. This is not weakness. It is power joined with patience."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Know that God knows what is within yourselves, so beware of Him. And know that God is Forgiving and Forbearing.” (2:235)\n> “If God were to hold people to account for what they earn, He would not leave on its surface a single creature.” (35:45)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He told a man from the tribe of Abd al-Qays: “You have two qualities that God loves: forbearance and deliberateness.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A Bedouin man came into the mosque and began to urinate in a corner of it. The companions rose to stop him. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Leave him alone, and pour a bucket of water over where he urinated. You were sent to make things easy, not to make things difficult.” (Bukhari, Muslim) Then he called the man and taught him, kindly, that mosques are for prayer and remembrance of God."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Next time you want to react, pause. Count slowly. Forbearance is what you choose when you could have exploded. Remember that God has been forbearing with you more times than you can count."}]},{"n":33,"tr":"Al-Azim","ar":"العظيم","m":"The Magnificent","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Azim is the Magnificent, the Mighty in greatness. His greatness is beyond our imagination, which is why the mind goes silent in front of it. Al-Kabir (the Most Great) is close in meaning, but Al-Azim leans toward awe and magnificence."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is the Most High, the Most Great.” (2:255, Ayat al-Kursi)\n> “So glorify the name of your Lord, the Magnificent.” (56:74)\n> “They did not appraise God with true appraisal. On the Day of Resurrection the whole earth will be in His grip, and the heavens will be folded in His right hand. Glory be to Him, and He is far above what they associate with Him.” (39:67)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Two phrases are light on the tongue, heavy on the scale, and beloved to the Most Merciful: *Subhan Allahi wa bihamdihi, Subhan Allahil-Azim* (Glory and praise to God; glory to God the Magnificent).” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nIn bowing (*ruku*) in prayer he would say: “*Subhana Rabbiyal-Azim*,” “Glory to my Lord, the Magnificent.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Stand under a night sky far from city lights. The Earth is a speck in it, and our galaxy is a speck in the universe. The One who holds all of it in His grip is called Al-Azim."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Say the *ruku* phrase slowly and feel the bowing. When a problem looks huge, say the name: nothing you face is bigger than the One who made the heavens and the earth."}]},{"n":34,"tr":"Al-Ghafur","ar":"الغفور","m":"The Oft-Forgiving","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Ghafur is the Oft-Forgiving. Where Al-Ghaffar stresses forgiving again and again, Al-Ghafur stresses how wide and deep His forgiveness is, so deep that the sin is covered and its consequences removed."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed, God forgives all sins. He is the Forgiving, the Merciful.” (39:53)\n> “Inform My servants that I am the Forgiving, the Merciful.” (15:49)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A man had killed ninety-nine people. He asked about the most knowledgeable person on earth and was sent to a monk. He asked, “Can I be forgiven?” The monk said no, so the man killed him too, making a hundred. He asked again and was sent to a scholar, who said, “Who can stand between you and repentance? Go to that land where people worship God, and worship with them. Do not go back to your own land, for it is an evil land.” He set out, but death came to him halfway. The angels of mercy and the angels of punishment argued over him. They were told to measure the distance between the two lands, and he was found to be a handspan closer to the land of the righteous. So the angels of mercy took him. (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Consider the worst thing you have done. Now remember that a man who had killed a hundred people was forgiven because he set out in sincerity. What you carry is not bigger than His forgiveness."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Never think a sin is too big to be forgiven. Turn back, take a step toward good, and change your surroundings if they pull you down. And forgive freely, the way you want to be forgiven."}]},{"n":35,"tr":"Ash-Shakur","ar":"الشكور","m":"The Appreciative","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ash-Shakur is the Appreciative: the One who notices small good deeds, thanks the doer, and rewards them far beyond what they were worth. He accepts a little effort and gives back a great deal."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “[He will give them] their rewards in full and increase them from His bounty. Indeed He is Forgiving and Appreciative.” (35:30)\n> “If you lend God a good loan, He will multiply it for you and forgive you. God is Appreciative and Forbearing.” (64:17)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “While a man was walking along a road, he found a thorny branch on the path and moved it aside. God appreciated this and forgave him.” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nHe used to stand in night prayer until his feet swelled. Aisha asked, “Why do you do this, when God has forgiven your past and future sins?” He said, “Should I not be a thankful servant?” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of how much a simple “thank you” from someone you admire lifts you. Now imagine it coming from the One who made everything, about something you thought nobody saw."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Do small good things without being asked, like moving the thorn. And thank people, because the Prophet ﷺ said, “Whoever does not thank people does not thank God.” (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi)"}]},{"n":36,"tr":"Al-Ali","ar":"العلي","m":"The Most High","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Ali is the Most High: He is above everything in rank, power and dignity. Nothing is above Him, and nothing is like Him. His highness does not make Him distant from us, because the Quran says He is also near."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is the Most High, the Most Great.” (2:255)\n> “Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High.” (87:1)\n> “Indeed God is Most High, Most Great.” (4:34)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In prostration (*sujud*) he would say, “*Subhana Rabbiyal-A’la*,” “Glory to my Lord, the Most High.” (Muslim)\nHe also said: “The closest a servant is to his Lord is when he is prostrating, so make plenty of supplication in it.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Notice the beautiful paradox of prayer. When your forehead is at the lowest point, on the ground, you are saying the name of the Highest. And that is where you are closest."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When you feel small or ignored, put your head down in sujud and say it slowly. You are not looking up to someone far away. You are bowing before the One who is closest to you."}]},{"n":37,"tr":"Al-Kabir","ar":"الكبير","m":"The Most Great","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Kabir is the Most Great: greater than anything we could compare Him to. Every Muslim says it many times a day in *Allahu Akbar*, which means “God is greater.” Greater than what? Greater than anything you are worried about, afraid of, or tempted by."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “[He is] the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the Most Great, the Exalted.” (13:9)\n> “That is because God is the Truth, and what they call on besides Him is falsehood, and God is the Most High, the Most Great.” (22:62)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “The most beloved words to God are four: *Subhan Allah, Alhamdulillah, La ilaha illa Allah*, and *Allahu Akbar*. It does not matter which one you begin with.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"The call to prayer begins with it, four times, every dawn, noon, afternoon, evening and night. Wherever you are in the world, someone is saying that God is greater."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Fill in the blank next time you feel pressure: “God is greater than my fear.” “God is greater than this deadline.” “God is greater than my pride.” Then say it out loud, and mean it."}]},{"n":38,"tr":"Al-Hafiz","ar":"الحفيظ","m":"The Preserver","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Hafiz is the Preserver and Guardian: the One who protects and keeps safe, who preserves every deed and every creature, and who keeps the heavens and earth from falling apart. Nothing can be lost to Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed We sent down the Reminder (the Quran), and indeed We will be its guardian.” (15:9)\n> “God is the best guardian, and He is the most merciful of the merciful.” (12:64, Yaqub)\n> “Indeed my Lord is Guardian over all things.” (11:57)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He told his young cousin Ibn Abbas: “Be mindful of God, and He will protect you. Be mindful of God, and you will find Him before you. If you ask, ask God. If you seek help, seek help from God.” (Tirmidhi)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Prophet ﷺ put Abu Hurayra in charge of guarding the charity stored in Ramadan. A thief came at night and took some food. Abu Hurayra caught him, and the man said, “I am needy and have dependents,” so he let him go. This happened three times. The third time the man said, “When you go to bed, recite Ayat al-Kursi. A guardian from God will stay with you and Satan will not come near you until morning.” When Abu Hurayra told the Prophet ﷺ, he said, “He told you the truth, even though he is a liar. That was Satan.” (Bukhari)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Say Ayat al-Kursi before sleep, and ask God to guard your family, your faith and your children. And be a guardian yourself: protect trusts, keep secrets, and look after what people leave with you."}]},{"n":39,"tr":"Al-Muqit","ar":"المقيت","m":"The Sustainer","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muqit is the Sustainer: the One who gives every creature the food and strength it needs to live, and who has power over everything, keeping it in balance. It comes from *qut*, daily food."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God is Sustainer over all things.” (4:85)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When Maryam (Mary) was in labor, alone and exhausted, God said to her, “Shake the trunk of the palm tree toward you, and it will drop fresh ripe dates for you. So eat, drink, and be comforted.” (19:23–26) God did not make her climb. He asked for a small movement, and sent what she needed in the moment she needed it."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Whoever among you wakes up safe in his home, healthy in his body, and with his food for the day, it is as if the whole world has been gathered for him.” (Tirmidhi)\nHe also said: “It is enough sin for a man to neglect those he is responsible for feeding.” (Abu Dawud, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be grateful for your ordinary morning: safety, health and a meal. And be a sustainer in your small circle, because nothing matters more to God than looking after those who depend on you."}]},{"n":40,"tr":"Al-Hasib","ar":"الحسيب","m":"The Reckoner","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Hasib is the Reckoner and the Sufficient: He takes full account of every deed, and He is enough for the one who relies on Him. The root *hasb* means both to count and to be enough."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Sufficient is God as Reckoner.” (4:6)\n> “Indeed God is ever a Reckoner over all things.” (4:86)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When Ibrahim (Abraham) was thrown into the fire, he said, “*Hasbunallahu wa ni’mal wakil*,” “Sufficient for us is God, and He is the best disposer of affairs.” And when the Prophet ﷺ and his companions, after Uhud, were told that the enemy had gathered against them, they said the same words. (Bukhari) The Quran says it increased their faith. (3:173)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"Aisha asked: “Doesn’t God say, ‘He will be reckoned with an easy reckoning’?” He said: “That is only the presentation of deeds. Whoever is questioned in detail is destroyed.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Say *Hasbunallah* when you are overwhelmed. And do a quiet review of your own day, before the day comes when it is reviewed for you."}]},{"n":41,"tr":"Al-Jalil","ar":"الجليل","m":"The Majestic","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Jalil is the Majestic: the One whose greatness, power and perfection are so complete that they fill the heart with awe. Majesty is what makes a person lower their voice.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. The Quran often uses the same root, as in “Possessor of Majesty and Honor” (55:27)."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “And there remains the Face of your Lord, Owner of Majesty and Honor.” (55:27)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Moses (Musa) asked to see his Lord. God said, “You will not see Me, but look at the mountain. If it stays in place, then you will see Me.” When his Lord revealed Himself to the mountain, it crumbled to dust, and Moses fell down unconscious. (7:143) If a mountain cannot bear a glimpse of His majesty, what are we?"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Part of honoring God is honoring a gray-haired Muslim, a bearer of the Quran who neither exaggerates in it nor neglects it, and a just ruler.” (Abu Dawud)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of standing at the foot of a huge mountain or before the open ocean, and how your voice drops and you go quiet. That feeling is a tiny echo of this name."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Bring awe into your prayer: stand like someone in front of a king. And honor what God has honored, the elderly, the Quran and fair leaders, because respecting them is part of respecting Him."}]},{"n":42,"tr":"Al-Karim","ar":"الكريم","m":"The Generous","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Karim is the Generous and the Noble. He gives without being asked, gives more than is deserved, and is not bothered by how much you take. He forgives when He could punish, and He treats you with dignity."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “O mankind, what has deceived you about your Lord, the Generous?” (82:6)\n> “Read, and your Lord is the Most Generous.” (96:3)\n> “Whoever is thankful is only thankful for his own good, and whoever is ungrateful, then indeed my Lord is Self-Sufficient and Generous.” (27:40)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Your Lord is Shy and Generous. He is shy before His servant, when the servant raises his hands to Him, to return them empty.” (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A man asked the Prophet ﷺ for sheep, and he gave him a whole flock that filled the space between two mountains. The man went back to his people and said, “O my people, accept Islam, for Muhammad gives like one who has no fear of poverty.” (Muslim) Anas said that a person might come to him wanting only the world, and by evening Islam would be dearer to him than the whole world. The Prophet’s ﷺ generosity was a reflection of his Lord’s."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Ask boldly: He is more generous than you imagine. And give the way you would like to receive, quickly and gracefully, without making anyone feel small."}]},{"n":43,"tr":"Ar-Raqib","ar":"الرقيب","m":"The Watchful","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ar-Raqib is the Watchful: He is always observing, never distracted, never sleeping. Nothing is ever out of His sight, including the thoughts of the heart. It is the reason a believer can act the same in private as in public."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed God is ever, over you, Watchful.” (4:1)\n> “[A person] does not utter a word except that there is an observer by him, ready.” (50:18)\n> “You were the Watcher over them [Jesus will say], and You are, over all things, a Witness.” (5:117)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Yusuf (Joseph) was alone in a locked room with the wife of the man who had taken him in. She closed the doors and called him to her. There was no one to see, no one to blame. Yusuf said, “God forbid! He is my master and has treated me well. Indeed the wrongdoers do not succeed.” (12:23) He knew Someone was watching, and that was enough to stop him."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Fear God wherever you are, follow a bad deed with a good one and it will erase it, and treat people with good character.” (Tirmidhi)\nAmong the seven shaded by God on the Day of Judgment is “a man invited by a woman of rank and beauty, who says: I fear God.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Make this your private rule: do right in secret. And when you fail in secret, return to Him quickly, because the same watching eye is a merciful one."}]},{"n":44,"tr":"Al-Mujib","ar":"المجيب","m":"The Responder","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mujib is the One who responds. When a person calls, He answers: sometimes with exactly what was asked, sometimes with something better, sometimes by saving it for later. He is never too busy to answer and never annoyed by repeated asking."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “When My servants ask you about Me, I am near. I respond to the call of the caller when he calls on Me.” (2:186)\n> “Indeed my Lord is near and responsive.” (11:61)\n> “Is He not the One who responds to the distressed when he calls on Him, and removes the evil?” (27:62)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Yunus (Jonah) was swallowed by a whale. In the three darknesses, of night, sea and the belly of the whale, he called out: “There is no god but You. Glory be to You. Indeed I was among the wrongdoers.” God says, “So We responded to him and saved him from the distress. And thus We save the believers.” (21:87–88) Ayyub (Job) called, “Adversity has touched me, and You are the most merciful of the merciful,” and God removed what troubled him. (21:83–84)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said that no Muslim calls with the dua of Yunus about anything but God answers him. (Tirmidhi)\nHe also said: “No Muslim makes a dua that has no sin and no cutting of family ties in it, but God gives him one of three things: He answers it quickly, He stores it for him in the Hereafter, or He turns away from him an equal harm.” The companions said, “Then we will make a lot of dua!” He said, “God has even more.” (Ahmad)\nAnd: “Your dua will be answered as long as you do not become impatient and say: I called but was not answered.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Keep asking, and don’t read the delay as a no. Every dua lands somewhere: now, later, or as protection from something you never saw."}]},{"n":45,"tr":"Al-Wasi","ar":"الواسع","m":"The Vast","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wasi is the Vast, the All-Encompassing: His mercy, knowledge, power and provision have no edge. No request is too big for Him, no group too large for His mercy, and nothing is outside His knowledge."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “To God belong the east and the west. Wherever you turn, there is the Face of God. Indeed God is All-Encompassing and All-Knowing.” (2:115)\n> “My mercy encompasses all things.” (7:156)\n> “Our Lord, You encompass all things in mercy and knowledge.” (40:7, the angels)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A Bedouin man was praying behind the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O God, have mercy on me and Muhammad, and do not have mercy on anyone else with us.” After the prayer, the Prophet ﷺ said to him, “You have restricted something that is wide.” (Bukhari) He meant God’s mercy. It is not a small thing to be rationed."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of the sky. You can point at one patch of it, but you can’t draw a line around all of it. That is a small picture of this name."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Pray big and pray widely, including for people you don’t know. And be wide yourself: wide in forgiveness, in generosity, and in giving people room to grow."}]},{"n":46,"tr":"Al-Hakim","ar":"الحكيم","m":"The Wise","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Hakim is the All-Wise: everything He decrees, allows or prevents has a purpose, even when we don’t see it. Wisdom means putting every thing in its right place at its right time, and He does this perfectly."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He gives wisdom to whom He wills, and whoever is given wisdom has been given much good.” (2:269)\n> “He is the All-Knowing, the All-Wise.” (12:83)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Musa (Moses) met a servant of God, al-Khidr, and asked to follow him to learn. Khidr damaged a boat belonging to poor people. He killed a young man. He rebuilt a wall in a town that refused them hospitality. Musa objected each time. Then Khidr explained: the boat was damaged so a king would not seize it, the young man would have grown into a danger to his believing parents, and the wall protected a buried treasure for two orphans. “I did not do it of my own accord,” said Khidr. (18:60–82) What looked like harm hid a wisdom Musa couldn’t see."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “How amazing is the affair of the believer: all of it is good for him, and this is true for no one but the believer. If good comes to him, he is thankful, and that is good for him. If harm comes to him, he is patient, and that is good for him.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"When something goes differently than planned, say: “Al-Hakim, I trust You.” Look for the wisdom later, and don’t force an explanation now."}]},{"n":47,"tr":"Al-Wadud","ar":"الودود","m":"The Loving","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wadud is the Loving: the One who loves His servants and whose love shows in what He gives. The Arabic form means the One who loves, and the One who is loved. His is a love that is real, steady, and intimate."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is the Forgiving, the Loving.” (85:14)\n> “Seek forgiveness of your Lord and then turn to Him. Indeed my Lord is Merciful and Loving.” (11:90)\n> “Those who believe and do righteous deeds, the Most Merciful will create love for them.” (19:96)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “When God loves a servant, He calls Gabriel and says: ‘I love so-and-so, so love him.’ Then Gabriel loves him and announces to the heavens: ‘God loves so-and-so, so love him.’ So the inhabitants of the heavens love him. Then acceptance is placed for him on the earth.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Prophet ﷺ took the hand of Mu’adh ibn Jabal and said, “O Mu’adh, by God, I love you. Do not forget to say at the end of every prayer: *O God, help me to remember You, to thank You, and to worship You well.*” (Abu Dawud, Nasa’i) A tender moment between a teacher and a student, and the teacher’s love was shown by giving him something to grow by."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Love does not need to be earned before it begins. Do the things God loves, and hold to the belief that He loves you. Then love His creation: “None of you truly believes until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"}]},{"n":48,"tr":"Al-Majid","ar":"المجيد","m":"The Glorious","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Majid is the Glorious: the One whose glory, honor and generosity are complete. It combines greatness with giving. A glorious king who is also kind."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Owner of the Throne, the Glorious.” (85:15)\n> “May the mercy of God and His blessings be upon you, people of the house. Indeed He is Praiseworthy and Glorious.” (11:73)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Angels came to Ibrahim (Abraham) and his wife Sarah to tell them they would have a child, Ishaq (Isaac), although they were old. Sarah was amazed: “Shall I give birth when I am an old woman?” The angels answered: “Are you amazed at God’s decree? May God’s mercy and blessings be upon you, people of the house. Indeed He is Praiseworthy and Glorious.” (11:69–73)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"This name ends the blessing on the Prophet ﷺ that we say in every prayer: “O God, send prayers upon Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, as You sent prayers upon Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Next time you say that ending in prayer, think of what you are saying. And aim to be glorious in the small sense: great in manners and generous in what you give."}]},{"n":49,"tr":"Al-Ba'ith","ar":"الباعث","m":"The Resurrector","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Ba’ith is the Resurrector: the One who raises the dead on the Day of Judgment, and who also sends messengers and awakens hearts. The same root gives the word for sending a prophet (*bi’tha*) and for waking someone up.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. The Quran says plainly: “God will resurrect those in the graves.” (22:7)"},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “And the Hour is coming, no doubt about it, and God will resurrect those in the graves.” (22:7)\n> “And We certainly sent into every nation a messenger.” (16:36)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A man passed by a town in ruins and wondered aloud how God could bring it to life after its death. God made him die for a hundred years, then raised him. “How long have you stayed?” “A day, or part of a day.” “No, you stayed a hundred years. Look at your food and drink, they have not spoiled. And look at your donkey.” Then he watched the donkey’s bones come together and be clothed with flesh. He said, “I know that God is over all things Capable.” (2:259)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"Each morning when he woke he would say: “All praise is for God who gave us life after causing us to die, and to Him is the resurrection.” (Bukhari) Every morning is a small rehearsal of the great one."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Say this dua when you wake up and think about the day when you will rise again. Let it keep you honest. And be a ba’ith yourself in a small way: wake up someone’s hope."}]},{"n":50,"tr":"Ash-Shahid","ar":"الشهيد","m":"The Witness","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ash-Shahid is the Witness: He sees and knows everything, as it happens, and He will testify to all of it. Nothing is ever lost. Every promise made, every wrong done, every kindness given, is witnessed."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God is sufficient as Witness.” (4:79)\n> “Is it not enough that your Lord is Witness over all things?” (41:53)\n> “Indeed God is, over all things, a Witness.” (22:17)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He told of a man on the Day of Judgment who will say, “O Lord, I will not accept any witness against me except from myself.” God will say, “Sufficient is your own self as a witness against you today, and the noble recording angels as witnesses.” Then his mouth will be sealed, and his limbs will be told to speak, and they will tell of his deeds. (Muslim) The Quran says the same: “Their hands will speak to Us, and their feet will testify.” (36:65)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Imagine every decision of your life played back, with nothing missing and nobody able to argue. For the wronged, it is relief. For the one who did wrong, it is reason to change now."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Tell the truth, even when it is hard: “Stand firm for justice as witnesses to God, even against yourselves.” (4:135) And if you carry a hurt that no one saw, remember that the Witness did."}]},{"n":51,"tr":"Al-Haqq","ar":"الحق","m":"The Truth","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Haqq is the Truth, the Real: the One whose existence is certain, whose word is true, whose promise is true, and whose judgment is true. Everything else depends on Him to exist. Falsehood is what has no real standing, and it fades."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “That is because God is the Truth, and it is He who gives life to the dead, and He is over all things Capable.” (22:6)\n> “Exalted is God, the True King.” (20:114)\n> “Truth has come and falsehood has vanished. Indeed falsehood is bound to vanish.” (17:81)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah in victory, there were three hundred and sixty idols around the Ka’ba. He pointed at them with a stick he had in his hand and recited, “Truth has come and falsehood has vanished,” and each one fell. (Bukhari, Muslim) The idols had stood for generations. Truth only needed to arrive."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In his night prayer he would say: “O God, to You belongs all praise. You are the Truth, Your promise is true, meeting You is true, Your word is true, Paradise is true, the Fire is true, the Prophets are true, and the Hour is true.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Choose the real over the flashy: truth in speech, honesty in work, certainty over rumors. And if you’ve built on something that is not true, you’ve got time to rebuild it."}]},{"n":52,"tr":"Al-Wakil","ar":"الوكيل","m":"The Trustee","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wakil is the Trustee, the One to whom you hand over your affairs and who manages them better than you could. To put your trust in Him (*tawakkul*) means to do your part, then rest your heart in His hands."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Whoever relies on God, He is sufficient for him.” (65:3)\n> “Sufficient for us is God, and He is the best disposer of affairs.” (3:173)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When Moses and the Children of Israel reached the sea with Pharaoh’s army behind them, his people said, “We are about to be overtaken!” Moses said, “No! My Lord is with me, and He will guide me.” (26:61–62) Then the sea was split. He said it before he saw how."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “If you relied on God as He should be relied on, He would provide for you as He provides for the birds: they leave in the morning hungry and return full.” (Tirmidhi)\nA man asked, “Should I tie my camel and trust in God, or leave it untied and trust in God?” He said, “Tie it, and trust in God.” (Tirmidhi) Trust is not laziness. It is effort with a calm heart."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Do the sensible thing, and then say *hasbunallah wa ni’mal wakil*. Let go of the outcome. You were only asked to try and to rely."}]},{"n":53,"tr":"Al-Qawi","ar":"القوي","m":"The Strong","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Qawi is the Strong: His power is complete, never tires and never weakens. Everything in the universe gets its strength from Him, so no one is strong on their own."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed God is Strong and Mighty.” (22:40)\n> “Did they not see that God, who created them, is greater than them in strength?” (41:15)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The people of ‘Ad were proud of their strength. They said, “Who is greater than us in power?” (41:15) God sent against them a fierce wind for seven nights and eight days, and they were left like hollow trunks of palm trees. (69:6–8) Their strength had been borrowed all along."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “The strong believer is better and more beloved to God than the weak believer, though there is good in both. Strive for what benefits you, seek help from God, and do not give up.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be strong in the ways that count: in your prayer, health, learning, patience and trustworthiness. And when you feel weak, remember where strength comes from."}]},{"n":54,"tr":"Al-Matin","ar":"المتين","m":"The Firm","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Matin is the Firm: His strength is steady, unshakable and unbreakable, and He is never moved by pressure. The word pairs strength with firmness, like a rope that can bear any load. He also makes His servants firm."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed it is God who is the Provider, the Possessor of firm strength, the Firm.” (51:58)\n> “God keeps firm those who believe with the firm word, in this world and in the Hereafter.” (14:27)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A king tried several times to kill a believing boy and failed. In the end the boy told him how it could be done: say “In the name of the Lord of this boy” as you shoot the arrow. The king did, the boy died, and the crowd cried out, “We believe in the Lord of the boy!” The furious king dug trenches, lit fires and threw the believers in. A woman holding her baby hesitated at the edge of the fire. Her baby spoke: “Mother, be patient, for you are on the truth.” (Muslim) The Quran mentions these believers as the People of the Ditch (85:4–8). Firmness is a gift from God."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A tree with deep roots is not blown over in a storm. That firm-rootedness is what Al-Matin gives."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Ask for firmness: “O God, I ask You for steadiness in all matters and determination to follow the right path.” (Ahmad, Nasa’i) And be a firm friend: keep your word and stay when it is hard."}]},{"n":55,"tr":"Al-Wali","ar":"الولي","m":"The Protecting Friend","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wali is the Protecting Friend: the One who looks after the believers, supports them, and stands close to them like a loyal ally. He takes them out of darkness into light. The Arabic root is about closeness and care."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God is the Ally of those who believe. He brings them out of darknesses into the light.” (2:257)\n> “Indeed, the friends of God, no fear will be upon them, nor will they grieve.” (10:62)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God says: “Whoever shows enmity to a friend of Mine, I declare war on him. My servant draws near to Me with nothing more beloved to Me than what I have made obligatory on him. And My servant keeps drawing near to Me with extra acts until I love him. When I love him, I become his hearing with which he hears, his sight with which he sees, his hand with which he strikes, and his foot with which he walks. If he asks Me, I will surely give him. If he seeks refuge in Me, I will surely protect him.” (Bukhari)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Picture someone who always has your back: they pick up the phone, they show up, they say the right thing. That is only a shadow of the protection of Al-Wali."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Become close to Him the way the hadith describes: do what is required, then add a little extra, such as a few more rak’ahs, a dua, or a charity. Then be a good wali to others: stand with those who have no one."}]},{"n":56,"tr":"Al-Hamid","ar":"الحميد","m":"The Praiseworthy","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Hamid is the Praiseworthy: He deserves all praise whether anyone praises Him or not. Everything He does is good, and everything good in creation points back to Him. Our praise adds nothing to Him. It is for our own good."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “O people, you are the ones in need of God, and God is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy.” (35:15)\n> “If you are ungrateful, then indeed God is Free of need and Praiseworthy.” (14:8)\nThe Quran opens with it: “All praise is for God, Lord of the worlds.” (1:2)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God is pleased with the servant who eats a meal and praises Him for it, or drinks a drink and praises Him for it.” (Muslim)\nHe also said: “*Alhamdulillah* fills the scale.” (Muslim)\nAnd when he saw something he disliked, he would say: “*Alhamdulillahi ‘ala kulli hal*,” “Praise be to God in every circumstance.” (Ibn Majah)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of thanking someone for the meal you ate. Now consider thanking the One who made the wheat grow, the rain fall and your body digest it."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Make *alhamdulillah* your response to both good and hard days. Praise changes the way you see what you have. And praise people too, because honest praise of good deeds is part of being thankful."}]},{"n":57,"tr":"Al-Muhsi","ar":"المحصي","m":"The Counter","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muhsi is the Counter: He has counted everything exactly, every grain of sand, every drop of rain, every breath, every deed. Nothing is forgotten or approximate.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. The Quran uses the root in verses like “He has counted everything in number” (72:28)."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He has encompassed what is with them, and has counted everything in number.” (72:28)\n> “God has counted it, while they forgot it.” (58:6)\n> “…the Book that leaves out nothing, small or great, without having counted it.” (18:49)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Spend, and do not keep count, or God will count against you.” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nThe same root appears in the hadith about the Names: “God has ninety-nine names. Whoever *ahsaha* them will enter Paradise.” (Bukhari, Muslim) Scholars say this word includes counting the names, learning them, understanding them and living by them."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"You couldn’t count the hairs on your own head. He has counted them on everyone, and every thought behind them."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Let this name make you careful about what you will have to see again. And give generously without keeping score, because the One who counts will account for it better than you."}]},{"n":58,"tr":"Al-Mubdi","ar":"المبدئ","m":"The Originator","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mubdi is the Originator: the One who starts creation. He began everything from nothing and still begins each new life, each new day and each new spring.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “He originates creation.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed it is He who originates and repeats.” (85:13)\n> “He originates creation, then repeats it.” (10:4)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God was, and there was nothing before Him. His Throne was upon the water. Then He created the heavens and the earth.” (Bukhari)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Look at a field in winter and again in spring. The dead-looking ground comes alive. The Quran gives exactly this picture: “You see the earth barren, then when We send down water upon it, it stirs and grows.” (41:39)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"When you need a new beginning, a new habit, a fresh start after a failure, remember that the Originator begins things all the time. Ask Him to begin something good in you today."}]},{"n":59,"tr":"Al-Mu'id","ar":"المعيد","m":"The Restorer","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mu’id is the Restorer: the One who brings back what He began. He restores life after death, a body after dust, and a heart after it has broken.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “He repeats it.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “As We began the first creation, We will repeat it.” (21:104)\n> “It is He who begins creation, then repeats it, and that is easier for Him.” (30:27)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Ibrahim (Abraham) said, “My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead.” God asked, “Do you not believe?” He said, “Yes, but so that my heart may be at ease.” God told him to take four birds, cut them up, place a part of each on separate hills, and then call them. They came to him flying. (2:260)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He once stood and said: “You will be gathered before God barefoot and unclothed,” and then recited: “As We began the first creation, We will repeat it.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Nothing is beyond restoring: a relationship, your faith, your health, your reputation. Ask the One who repeats creation to repair what feels past repair."}]},{"n":60,"tr":"Al-Muhyi","ar":"المحيي","m":"The Giver of Life","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muhyi is the Giver of Life: the One who brings the dead earth back to life, gives life to every living thing, and revives hearts that have gone cold. Life is on loan from Him.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “He gives life and causes death.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “My Lord is the One who gives life and causes death.” (2:258, Ibrahim)\n> “So look at the effects of God’s mercy, how He gives life to the earth after its death. Indeed that One is the Giver of life to the dead.” (30:50)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Ibrahim argued with a king who claimed to be a god. Ibrahim said, “My Lord is the One who gives life and causes death.” The king said, “I give life and cause death,” and had one man freed and another executed. Ibrahim replied, “God brings the sun from the east, so bring it from the west.” The disbeliever was stunned. (2:258) Giving life is not just a claim. It is the one thing no ruler can do."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “The example of the one who remembers his Lord and the one who does not is like the living and the dead.” (Bukhari)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Give life to your heart through remembrance, and to your surroundings through kindness. Be a small *muhyi*: plant, comfort, teach, revive."}]},{"n":61,"tr":"Al-Mumit","ar":"المميت","m":"The Giver of Death","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mumit is the Giver of Death: the One who ends each life at its appointed time. Death is not an accident or a failure. It is a decree, and it is a doorway from this life to the next.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “He gives life and causes death.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He who created death and life to test you, which of you is best in deed.” (67:2)\n> “Say: The death from which you flee will surely meet you.” (62:8)\n> “It is He who gives life and causes death.” (53:44)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He took Ibn Umar by the shoulder and said: “Be in this world as though you were a stranger or a traveler.” (Bukhari)\nHe also said: “Remember often the destroyer of pleasures: death.” (Tirmidhi, Nasa’i)\nBefore sleep, which is a small death, he would say: “In Your name, O God, I die and I live.” (Bukhari)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of a hotel room. You enjoy it, you use it well, you keep it tidy, but you know you’re checking out. This world is that room."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Remembering death is not meant to make you sad. It is meant to make you honest. Mend a relationship, pay back what you owe, and write down what you want done. Then live well, with gratitude."}]},{"n":62,"tr":"Al-Hayy","ar":"الحي","m":"The Ever-Living","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Hayy is the Ever-Living: His life has no beginning and no end, needs nothing, and gives life to all else. Everything that lives, lives by His leave. He never sleeps, tires or dies."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God, there is no god but Him, the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining.” (2:255)\n> “Rely on the Ever-Living who does not die.” (25:58)\n> “He is the Ever-Living. There is no god but Him.” (40:65)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When the Prophet ﷺ died, many companions could not believe it, and Umar was in shock. Abu Bakr came, went to the Prophet’s ﷺ body and kissed him, then went out and addressed the people: “Whoever used to worship Muhammad, Muhammad has died. And whoever used to worship God, God is Ever-Living and never dies.” Then he recited, “Muhammad is only a messenger. Messengers have passed away before him.” (3:144) (Bukhari) In the greatest grief of the community, this name held them up."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He used to say: “O God, I seek refuge in Your might. There is no god but You, who never dies, while the jinn and mankind die.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Everyone you love will pass away, and so will you. Attach your heart most to the One who doesn’t. When you’re in need, call on Him: “O Ever-Living, O Self-Sustaining.”"}]},{"n":63,"tr":"Al-Qayyum","ar":"القيوم","m":"The Self-Sustaining","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Qayyum is the Self-Sustaining: He exists by Himself, needs nothing to keep Him going, and keeps everything else going. If His support were withdrawn for a moment, everything would collapse. He also manages and stands over every affair."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God, there is no god but Him, the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining. Neither drowsiness nor sleep overtakes Him.” (2:255)\n> “And faces will be humbled before the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining.” (20:111)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He once asked Ubayy ibn Ka’b which verse of the Quran is the greatest. Ubayy answered, “God, there is no god but Him, the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining,” (Ayat al-Kursi). The Prophet ﷺ struck him on the chest and said, “May knowledge be easy for you, Abu al-Mundhir.” (Muslim)\nWhen something distressed him, he would say: “O Ever-Living, O Self-Sustaining, in Your mercy I seek relief.” (Tirmidhi)\nHe also taught his daughter Fatima to say each morning and evening: “O Ever-Living, O Self-Sustaining, in Your mercy I seek relief. Set right all my affairs and do not leave me to myself, even for the blink of an eye.” (al-Nasa’i, al-Hakim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A lamp only shines while it’s plugged into a source. Everything in the universe is like that, and Al-Qayyum is the source that never runs out."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Learn Ayat al-Kursi by heart. Say the dua taught to Fatima, and mean the last part: you really can’t manage your life alone, not for the blink of an eye."}]},{"n":64,"tr":"Al-Wajid","ar":"الواجد","m":"The Finder","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wajid is the Finder: the One who finds whatever He wills, who never lacks anything, and from whom nothing can hide. He is rich in every way and cannot be missing anything.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list, from the root *wajada*, “to find.” The Quran uses this root in a beautiful verse about the Prophet ﷺ (93:6–8)."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Did He not find you an orphan and give you refuge? Did He not find you lost and guide you? Did He not find you in need and make you rich?” (93:6–8)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A person walking through a desert on his camel, carrying all his food and drink, falls asleep. He wakes and the camel has gone. He searches until he is almost dead from thirst and lies down to die. Then he opens his eyes and the camel is standing next to him. In his joy he cries, “O God, You are my servant and I am Your Lord,” a slip of the tongue from happiness. The Prophet ﷺ said that God is even happier with the repentance of His servant than that man. (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"When you’re lost, you are not lost to Him. In the verse above, He found the Prophet ﷺ as an orphan, lost and in need, and gave him refuge, guidance and enough."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Whatever you’ve lost, whether direction, a loved one or hope, pray to the Finder. And when you find what you were looking for, thank the One who let you find it."}]},{"n":65,"tr":"Al-Majid","ar":"الماجد","m":"The Noble","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Majid (in this form) is the Noble, the One whose glory is shown in how generously He acts. Some lists give two names from this root: المجيد (*Al-Majid*, the Glorious) and الماجد (*Al-Majid*, the Noble). They are very close in meaning. Both come from *majd*, glory joined with generosity.\nNote: this form comes from the traditional list. The prayer hadith below uses the same root."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “We have honored the children of Adam.” (17:70)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God says: “I have divided the prayer between Myself and My servant, and My servant will have what he asks. When the servant says, ‘All praise is for God, Lord of the worlds,’ God says, ‘My servant has praised Me.’ When he says, ‘The Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate,’ God says, ‘My servant has extolled Me.’ When he says, ‘Master of the Day of Judgment,’ God says, ‘My servant has glorified Me.’” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Every time you recite the Fatiha, you are having a conversation, and God answers each line. At “Master of the Day of Judgment,” He says you have glorified Him."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Slow down in the Fatiha next time. Listen for the replies. And try to be noble in the way you treat people: generous in what you give, and gracious in how you give it."}]},{"n":66,"tr":"Al-Wahid","ar":"الواحد","m":"The One","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wahid is the One: He has no partner, no equal, no rival, and no one shares His divinity. It means oneness of essence. There is one Creator, one Lord, and one who deserves worship."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Your God is One God. There is no god but Him, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate.” (2:163)\n> “Say: God is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the Subduer.” (13:16)\n> “The Day the earth will be changed into another earth… and they will come out before God, the One, the Subduer.” (14:48)"},{"h":"Story","t":"As a young man, Ibrahim (Abraham) looked up at the night sky. He saw a star and said, “This is my lord,” but when it set he said, “I do not love those that set.” He saw the moon rising and said the same, and when it set, he said, “If my Lord does not guide me, I will be among the people who go astray.” Then he saw the sun, bigger and brighter, and when it set too he said, “O my people, I am free of what you associate. I have turned my face to the One who created the heavens and the earth.” (6:76–79)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"Mu’adh was riding behind him on a donkey when he asked, “Do you know the right of God over His servants, and the right of the servants over God?” Mu’adh said, “God and His Messenger know best.” He said, “The right of God over His servants is that they worship Him and associate nothing with Him. And the right of the servants over God is that He does not punish whoever associates nothing with Him.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Oneness frees you. You answer to one Lord, not to what people think. Let God be your first and final reference point."}]},{"n":67,"tr":"Al-Ahad","ar":"الأحد","m":"The Unique","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Ahad is the Unique: not only one in number, but one in kind. Nothing is like Him, and He cannot be divided, split or compared. It is the very first attribute of God in Surah Al-Ikhlas."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Say: He is God, the One. God, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born, and there is nothing comparable to Him.” (112:1–4)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said that Surah Al-Ikhlas is equal to a third of the Quran. (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Prophet ﷺ sent a man to lead a group, and whenever he led them in prayer, he ended his recitation with Surah Al-Ikhlas. When they returned and told the Prophet ﷺ, he said, “Ask him why he does that.” The man said, “Because it is the description of the Most Merciful, and I love to recite it.” The Prophet ﷺ said, “Tell him that God loves him.” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nIt is also reported that when Bilal was being tortured for his faith, he would say only, “*Ahad, Ahad*,” “One, One.” (Ahmad, Ibn Majah)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Recite Surah Al-Ikhlas with love, as that man did. And let this name simplify your life: if God is One, your heart can have one real center."}]},{"n":68,"tr":"As-Samad","ar":"الصمد","m":"The Eternal Refuge","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"As-Samad is the Eternal Refuge: the One to whom everyone turns in need, while He needs no one. In Arabic *samad* is also something solid with no hollow inside, so that nothing can get in or out of it. He is complete."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God, the Eternal Refuge.” (112:2)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Ibrahim (Abraham) brought his wife Hajar and their baby Ismail to a barren valley in Arabia, with nothing but a bag of dates and a skin of water. He turned and began to leave. Hajar followed him, asking, “Ibrahim, where are you going, leaving us in a valley with no one and nothing?” She asked again and again, but he did not answer. Finally she asked, “Did God command you to do this?” He said, “Yes.” She said, “Then He will not abandon us.” (Bukhari) Soon she was running between the hills of Safa and Marwa in search of water, and the spring of Zamzam burst forth. Pilgrims still retrace her steps today."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of the person you call first when things go wrong. Now picture a call that no one else can answer, and that always connects."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When everyone else is unable to help, turn to As-Samad. And be a *samad* in a small way for people near you: be someone they can turn to."}]},{"n":69,"tr":"Al-Qadir","ar":"القادر","m":"The Able","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Qadir is the Able: He has power over everything, and nothing is impossible for Him. Where Al-Qawi stresses strength, Al-Qadir stresses ability: whatever He wants, He can do."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed God is over all things Capable.” (2:20)\n> “Is not He who created the heavens and the earth able to create the like of them?” (36:81)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Maryam (Mary) was stunned when told she would have a son. “My Lord, how can I have a child when no man has touched me?” The answer was: “Such is God. He creates what He wills. When He decrees something, He only says to it, ‘Be,’ and it is.” (3:47)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In the prayer for guidance (*istikhara*) he taught us to say: “You have power and I have none. You know and I do not know.” (Bukhari)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"When your problem looks impossible, remember the question isn’t whether you can do it, but whether He can. And keep your own hope big: you are asking the One for whom nothing is out of reach."}]},{"n":70,"tr":"Al-Muqtadir","ar":"المقتدر","m":"The All-Powerful","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muqtadir is the All-Powerful: His power is complete, perfect and always effective. The form of the name stresses that His ability is never held back. If He decides something, it happens."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “We seized them with the seizing of one Almighty, Perfect in Ability.” (54:42)\n> “God is, over all things, Perfect in Ability.” (18:45)\n> “In a seat of honor, near a Sovereign, Perfect in Ability.” (54:55)"},{"h":"Story","t":"In the year the Prophet ﷺ was born, a ruler named Abraha marched on Makkah with an army and an elephant to destroy the Ka’ba. The people of Makkah had no power to resist. The Quran says God sent flocks of birds carrying stones of baked clay, “and He made them like chewed-up straw.” (105:1–5) The great army was defeated without a single Makkan fighting."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"The strongest army in the world is a pile of straw to the One who is Perfect in Ability."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When you face something far bigger than you, such as a powerful person, a mountain of debt or a diagnosis, remember who is truly in charge. Do what you can, and rest the rest on Al-Muqtadir."}]},{"n":71,"tr":"Al-Muqaddim","ar":"المقدم","m":"The Advancer","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muqaddim is the One who brings forward: He moves some things, people and events to the front, and puts them ahead of others. He advances whom He wills in rank, in time and in honor, with perfect wisdom.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. It is paired with Al-Mu’akhkhir in the Prophet’s ﷺ own words, below."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “And the forerunners, the forerunners. Those are the ones brought near.” (56:10–11)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"At the end of his night prayer he would say: “O God, forgive me what I have done before and what I have done after, what I did in secret and what I did openly, and what You know better than I. You are the Advancer and You are the Delayer. There is no god but You.” (Muslim)\nHe also said: “We are the last, yet we will be the first on the Day of Resurrection.” (Bukhari, Muslim) The final community is brought to the front."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of a race where the finishing order is decided by Someone who sees every runner’s heart, not just their speed."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Don’t envy those who seem ahead of you. Your place is set by the One who advances perfectly. Race in good deeds, though, because the Quran praises those who are *first* in goodness."}]},{"n":72,"tr":"Al-Mu'akhkhir","ar":"المؤخر","m":"The Delayer","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mu’akhkhir is the One who puts back and delays: He holds some things back until the right moment, in times, results, answers to dua, and people’s fates. Delay is not denial. It is timing.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “When their term comes, they cannot delay it by an hour, nor advance it.” (16:61)\n> “Indeed, the term set by God, when it comes, will not be delayed, if only you knew.” (71:4)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Zakariyya (Zechariah) made dua for a child for many years. He was old, his bones weak, his hair white, and his wife unable to have children. Yet he said to his Lord, “I have never been unhappy in calling on You, my Lord.” (19:3–4) The answer was delayed, but it came, with a son named Yahya (John). (19:7)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He ended his prayers with: “You are the Advancer and You are the Delayer.” (Muslim) The delaying and the advancing are both His, and both are good."},{"h":"Live it","t":"If something you’ve asked for hasn’t come, ask yourself whether it is delayed because the timing is not ready. And don’t delay what is in your hands: repentance, kindness, a phone call you owe."}]},{"n":73,"tr":"Al-Awwal","ar":"الأول","m":"The First","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Awwal is the First: He was before everything, and there was nothing before Him. He has no beginning. Everything else came after Him, and exists because of Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden, and He is Knowing of all things.” (57:3)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God was, and there was nothing before Him.” (Bukhari)\nHe used to teach this dua for bedtime: “O God, Lord of the heavens and Lord of the earth and Lord of the Mighty Throne. You are the First, and there is nothing before You. You are the Last, and there is nothing after You. You are the Manifest, and there is nothing above You. You are the Hidden, and there is nothing closer than You. Settle our debts for us, and free us from poverty.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Try to imagine what came before time. You can’t, and that’s alright. The name asks you only to know that He was already there."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Make Him your first: first thought on waking, first call in trouble, first thanks. If He is the First in your day, everything else falls into its place."}]},{"n":74,"tr":"Al-Akhir","ar":"الآخر","m":"The Last","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Akhir is the Last: everything else will pass away, and He remains. When the last of creation is gone, He is still there, as He was before. Nothing comes after Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Everyone upon it (the earth) will perish, and there will remain the Face of your Lord, Owner of Majesty and Honor.” (55:26–27)\n> “Everything will perish except His Face.” (28:88)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In the bedtime dua: “You are the Last, and there is nothing after You.” (Muslim)\nHe also said: “Whoever’s last words are ‘There is no god but God’ will enter Paradise.” (Abu Dawud)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of a place you loved as a child. The people who filled it have mostly gone. Everything you can see will go the same way. The only thing that was there at the start, and will be at the end, is the One who is First and Last."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Aim your life at the Last. Ask Him for a good ending, and practice for it by saying the shahada often, with sincerity."}]},{"n":75,"tr":"Az-Zahir","ar":"الظاهر","m":"The Manifest","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Az-Zahir is the Manifest: His existence is more obvious than anything else. Every sign in creation points to Him, and nothing is above Him in power and dominance. He is not hidden because He is faint. He is hidden only because He is so near and so great."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “He is the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden.” (57:3)\n> “We will show them Our signs in the horizons and within themselves until it becomes clear to them that it is the truth.” (41:53)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In the bedtime dua: “You are the Manifest, and there is nothing above You.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Everything you see is a sign: a leaf, a birth, a sunrise. We can’t see the sun at midday by staring at it, but everything else is visible by it. That’s the shape of this name."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Read the world as a book that points to its Author. Slow down in front of anything beautiful and let it lead you to Him."}]},{"n":76,"tr":"Al-Batin","ar":"الباطن","m":"The Hidden","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Batin is the Hidden: we cannot see Him with our eyes in this world, but He is nearer to us than anything. He knows the innermost secrets, and nothing is concealed from Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “We are closer to him than his jugular vein.” (50:16)\n> “He is with you wherever you are.” (57:4)\n> “There is no secret conversation of three but He is the fourth of them, nor of five but He is the sixth.” (58:7)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Yunus (Jonah) was in three layers of darkness: the night, the sea and the belly of the whale. No one on earth knew where he was. Yet he called out, and he was heard. (21:87) If He was close enough to hear Yunus there, He is close enough to hear you."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In the bedtime dua: “You are the Hidden, and there is nothing closer than You.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Nothing in you is hidden from Him, so you can drop the act, and say what you really feel. He is the closest listener you will ever have."}]},{"n":77,"tr":"Al-Wali","ar":"الوالي","m":"The Governor","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Wali (in this form) is the Governor: the One who manages and runs all affairs, and no one shares His rule. This is a different name from the Protecting Friend (الولي). It is about authority. He alone governs the universe and your life.\nNote: this form comes from the traditional list. The Quran says of the people, “They have no governor besides Him.” (13:11)"},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “They have no ally (governor) besides Him, and He shares His rule with no one.” (13:11, 18:26)\n> “God prevails over His affair, but most people do not know.” (12:21)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Yusuf (Joseph) was thrown into a well by his brothers, sold into slavery, and bought by a man in Egypt. At that point of the story the Quran stops to say: “God prevails over His affair, but most people do not know.” (12:21) The brothers thought they were in charge. The Governor was moving every piece."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He would pray: “O God, set right for me my religion, which is the safeguard of my affairs. Set right for me my worldly life, in which is my livelihood. Set right for me my Hereafter, to which is my return. Make life an increase for me in every good, and make death a rest for me from every evil.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Give God the keys to your affairs, not only the ones you feel stuck on. Make this dua a habit, and trust the Governor with the outcomes."}]},{"n":78,"tr":"Al-Muta'ali","ar":"المتعالي","m":"The Self-Exalted","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muta’ali is the Self-Exalted: He is far above anything we can describe or imagine, above every flaw, and above being compared with anything. His exaltedness belongs to Him by Himself and not through anyone."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “[He is] the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the Most Great, the Self-Exalted.” (13:9)\n> “There is nothing like Him, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.” (42:11)\n> “Exalted is He and high above what they say, by a great height.” (17:43)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"The opening words of the prayer: “Glory and praise to You, O God. Blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god but You.” (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi) The word “exalted” here comes from the same root as this name."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"The best poem, the greatest speech, and the deepest thought are all words made by human beings. They fall short, always. That is the sense of a God who is above all description, and why we say *Subhan Allah*."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When your mind goes to “what is God like?”, stop at what He told us about Himself. Spend the energy on what He asked of you. Praise, and then be silent in awe."}]},{"n":79,"tr":"Al-Barr","ar":"البر","m":"The Source of Goodness","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Barr is the Source of Goodness: the One who is kind, gentle and generous to all of creation, and who does good for its own sake. The same word, *birr*, is used for being dutiful and kind to parents and for righteousness in general."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed we used to call upon Him before. Indeed it is He who is the Source of Goodness, the Merciful.” (52:28, said by the people of Paradise)\n> “You will never attain righteousness (*birr*) until you spend from what you love.” (3:92)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"Ibn Mas’ud asked: “Which deed is most beloved to God?” He said, “Prayer at its proper time.” “Then which?” “Being good (*birr*) to parents.” “Then which?” “Striving in the way of God.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Three men were trapped in a cave when a boulder rolled across the entrance. Each prayed by mentioning his best deed done only for God. The first said that every night he brought milk to his elderly parents before his family, and one night he found them asleep. He stood with the bowl in his hand all night, not drinking and not waking them, until dawn. After each prayer the rock moved a little, until they walked out. (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be *barr* with your parents while they are alive, and after they pass, with prayers and good deeds for them. And do good simply because it is good."}]},{"n":80,"tr":"At-Tawwab","ar":"التواب","m":"The Acceptor of Repentance","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"At-Tawwab is the Acceptor of Repentance, the One who turns toward His servant again and again. Notice the double movement: He lets you turn back, and He turns back to you. He gives you the chance to repent, accepts it when you do, and is happy about it."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Then Adam received words from his Lord, and He accepted his repentance. Indeed it is He who is the Acceptor of repentance, the Merciful.” (2:37)\n> “Indeed God loves those who repent and those who purify themselves.” (2:222)\n> “O you who believe, repent to God with sincere repentance.” (66:8)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Adam and Hawwa (Eve) ate from the tree they were told not to. They said, “Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will surely be among the losers.” (7:23) God accepted their repentance. Compare it to Iblis, who also disobeyed but never said sorry. The difference between them is not the mistake. It is what each did next."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “All the children of Adam make mistakes, and the best of those who make mistakes are those who repent.” (Tirmidhi, Ibn Majah)\nHe also said: “Whoever repents before the sun rises from the west, God will accept his repentance.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Don’t wait until you feel good enough to turn back. Do it now, with a plain, honest sentence: stop, regret, and resolve not to return. The door is open until the sun rises from the west."}]},{"n":81,"tr":"Al-Muntaqim","ar":"المنتقم","m":"The Avenger","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muntaqim is the Avenger, the One who holds wrongdoers to account when they persist in injustice. It is not a petty or hot-tempered revenge. It is justice, given late and fair, to those who refuse to stop oppressing. For the oppressed, it is a promise.\nNote: the Quran does not use this as a stand-alone title. It says “God is Almighty, Owner of retribution (*Dhu intiqam*)” (3:4) and “We will take retribution (*muntaqimun*) from the criminals” (32:22). The name comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed, from the criminals We will take retribution.” (32:22)\n> “Such is the seizure of your Lord when He seizes the towns while they are doing wrong. Indeed His seizure is painful and severe.” (11:102)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “God gives the wrongdoer respite, but when He seizes him, He does not let him go.” Then he recited the verse above (11:102). (Bukhari, Muslim)\nWhen sending Mu’adh to Yemen, he said: “Beware of the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no veil between it and God.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Aisha said that the Prophet ﷺ never took revenge for anything done to himself, but if the limits of God were violated, he would act for God’s sake. (Bukhari, Muslim) Think of all the harm he was shown in Makkah and Ta’if, and how he responded."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Leave vengeance to Him. It frees you. Never be the oppressor, because the oppressed have a direct line. And don’t let a name about justice make you hard-hearted: God’s mercy outweighs His anger."}]},{"n":82,"tr":"Al-Afuww","ar":"العفو","m":"The Pardoner","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Afuww is the Pardoner: He wipes the slate clean. Forgiveness (*maghfira*) covers the sin. Pardon (*afw*) erases it, so that it is as if it was never written. He pardons even when He could punish."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “If you do good openly or in secret, or pardon an evil, then indeed God is Pardoning and Capable.” (4:149)\n> “Let them pardon and overlook. Would you not like God to forgive you?” (24:22)\n> “Whoever pardons and makes peace, his reward is with God.” (42:40)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"Aisha asked: “If I know which night is the Night of Power, what should I say?” He said: “Say: *Allahumma innaka ‘afuwwun tuhibbul-‘afwa fa’fu ‘anni*, O God, You are Pardoning and You love to pardon, so pardon me.” (Tirmidhi, Ibn Majah)"},{"h":"Story","t":"After the slander against Aisha, Abu Bakr stopped supporting his poor relative Mistah, who had joined those spreading the rumor. Then God revealed, “Let them pardon and overlook. Would you not like God to forgive you?” Abu Bakr said, “Yes, by God, I would love for God to forgive me,” and he resumed the support. (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Pardon someone who doesn’t deserve it. It is the best way to say the dua above. Pardon what you can, in the family, at work, with those who hurt you."}]},{"n":83,"tr":"Ar-Ra'uf","ar":"الرؤوف","m":"The Compassionate","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ar-Ra’uf is the Compassionate: mercy at its most tender. It is the gentle softness that wants to spare someone every hardship it can. Ar-Rahim gives mercy. Ar-Ra’uf is mercy that feels for the one it helps."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “There has come to you a messenger from among yourselves. It grieves him that you suffer. He is anxious for you, and to the believers he is kind and merciful.” (9:128)\n> “Indeed God is, to people, Kind and Merciful.” (2:143)\n> “God warns you of Himself, and God is Kind to His servants.” (3:30)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Prophet ﷺ said: “I begin the prayer wanting to make it long, and then I hear a child crying, so I shorten it, because I know how much his mother is troubled by his crying.” (Bukhari, Muslim) The Messenger who was described with this name by his Lord was stopping a prayer for a baby."},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of how a parent stops everything when a child is in pain. Compassion that has no other aim but your ease is this name."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When you see someone struggling, ask yourself: “What could I make easier for them?” Shorten a task, soften a word, share a burden. That is how mercy becomes ra’fa."}]},{"n":84,"tr":"Malik-ul-Mulk","ar":"مالك الملك","m":"Owner of All Sovereignty","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Malik-ul-Mulk is the Owner of All Sovereignty. Al-Malik is the King. This name says that He is the owner of the kingship itself, of all kingdoms and kings. Every throne, crown and power is on loan from Him, and He gives or takes as He wills."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Say: O God, Owner of Sovereignty. You give sovereignty to whom You will and take it away from whom You will. You honor whom You will and You humble whom You will. In Your hand is all good. Indeed You are over all things Capable.” (3:26)\n> “You make the night enter the day and the day enter the night. You bring the living out of the dead and the dead out of the living, and You provide for whom You will without account.” (3:27)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Children of Israel asked their prophet to appoint a king. He told them God had chosen Talut (Saul). They objected: “How can he have kingship over us when we are more deserving of it, and he was not given abundant wealth?” The prophet said, “God has chosen him over you, and increased him in knowledge and in stature. God gives His kingdom to whom He wills.” (2:246–247) Later a young man named Dawud (David) defeated the giant Jalut (Goliath), and “God gave him kingship and wisdom.” (2:251)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God will fold the heavens and the earth in His hand and say: “I am the King. Where are the kings of the earth?” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Ask Him for what you need without worrying whether you’re “important” enough. And don’t cling to rank or titles: you hold them on loan."}]},{"n":85,"tr":"Dhul-Jalali wal-Ikram","ar":"ذو الجلال والإكرام","m":"Lord of Majesty and Generosity","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Dhul-Jalali wal-Ikram is the Lord of Majesty and Generosity. It joins two things: *Jalal*, the greatness that makes us humble, and *Ikram*, the honor and generosity He pours on His servants. He is awesome, and He is kind."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Blessed is the name of your Lord, Owner of Majesty and Honor.” (55:78)\n> “And there remains the Face of your Lord, Owner of Majesty and Honor.” (55:27)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"The Prophet ﷺ heard a man praying: “O God, I ask You, for to You belongs all praise. There is no god but You, the Bestower, Originator of the heavens and the earth, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor, O Ever-Living, O Self-Sustaining.” He said: “He has asked God by His greatest name, which when He is called by it, He answers, and when He is asked by it, He gives.” (Abu Dawud, Tirmidhi)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Imagine meeting a great king who is also the kindest person you have ever met. You would bow, and you would also feel safe."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Open your dua with the names of God that fit what you need, including this one. Balance awe with love: be humble before Him and hopeful in Him."}]},{"n":86,"tr":"Al-Muqsit","ar":"المقسط","m":"The Equitable","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Muqsit is the Equitable: the One who gives each person their due, treating everyone fairly, and who defends the rights of the weak against the strong. It is like Al-Adl, but with a focus on putting things in balance and being even-handed.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. The Quran says, “God loves the just (*muqsitin*).” (5:42)"},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God witnesses that there is no god but Him, and so do the angels and those of knowledge, upholding justice.” (3:18)\n> “Indeed God loves those who act justly.” (5:42)\n> “O you who believe, stand firmly for justice, as witnesses for God, even if it is against yourselves, your parents or your relatives.” (4:135)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “The just will be on pulpits of light beside the Most Merciful, at His right hand. They are those who are just in their rulings, with their families, and in what they have been put in charge of.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A man named Bashir wanted to give a gift to one of his sons and asked the Prophet ﷺ to witness it. The Prophet ﷺ asked, “Have you given the same to all your children?” He said no. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Fear God and be just among your children,” and refused to be a witness. (Bukhari, Muslim) Even the gift to a child has to be fair."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Look for places where you treat people unequally without noticing: among children, colleagues, friends. Make it a habit to weigh with an even scale."}]},{"n":87,"tr":"Al-Jami","ar":"الجامع","m":"The Gatherer","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Jami is the Gatherer: He gathers people on the Day of Judgment, brings hearts together in love, and joins together everything needed for a purpose. All of creation, from the first human to the last, will be gathered in one place before Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Our Lord, You will gather mankind for a Day about which there is no doubt.” (3:9)\n> “Wherever you may be, God will bring you all together.” (2:148)"},{"h":"Story","t":"In Madinah, the tribes of Aws and Khazraj had fought each other for generations. When Islam came, God joined their hearts. The Quran reminds them: “Remember God’s favor upon you when you were enemies, and He brought your hearts together, so you became brothers by His favor.” (3:103) The Prophet ﷺ said: “The believers, in their love, mercy and compassion for one another, are like one body. If one part complains, the whole body responds with sleeplessness and fever.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A hundred strangers waiting in an airport can become a group when the flight is canceled. The One who can gather every human in one place can certainly gather hearts."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Be a person who brings people together: reconcile a dispute, introduce two people who need each other, or invite someone to the table. Avoid being the one who splits."}]},{"n":88,"tr":"Al-Ghani","ar":"الغني","m":"The Self-Sufficient","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Ghani is the Self-Sufficient: He needs nothing and nobody. All of creation needs Him, but nothing we do can add to Him or take away. The one who is rich beyond need can give without any loss."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “O mankind, you are the ones in need of God, and God is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy.” (35:15)\n> “God is the Free of need, and you are the needy.” (47:38)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God says: “O My servants, if the first of you and the last of you, the humans among you and the jinn, were all as pious as the most pious heart among you, it would not add anything to My kingdom. And if all of you were as wicked as the most wicked heart among you, it would not take anything from My kingdom. If the first of you and the last, the humans and the jinn, stood on one plain and all asked of Me, and I gave each what he asked, it would not decrease what I have, except as a needle decreases the sea when it is dipped into it.” (Muslim)\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said: “Richness is not having many possessions. Richness is the richness of the soul.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Feel the freedom of being needy before Him. It is the only need that never shames you. And aim for the richness of the soul: enough is enough, and contentment is a kind of wealth."}]},{"n":89,"tr":"Al-Mughni","ar":"المغني","m":"The Enricher","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mughni is the Enricher: the One who makes a person truly rich, in money, yes, but also in heart, health and contentment. He gives what removes need. Al-Ghani needs nothing. Al-Mughni makes others need less.\nNote: the Quran uses this as a verb, “He enriches.” The name itself comes from the traditional list."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “And that it is He who enriches and gives contentment.” (53:48)\n> “Did He not find you in need and make you rich?” (93:8)\n> “If you fear poverty, God will enrich you from His bounty if He wills.” (9:28)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Successful is the one who has submitted, has been given enough provision, and whom God has made content with what He gave him.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"The Prophet ﷺ told of three men of the Children of Israel: one had leprosy, one was bald, and one was blind. God sent an angel to each and gave them what they asked: healthy skin, hair, and sight. They were also given wealth in camels, cows and sheep. Later the angel came back in disguise, as a poor traveler, to ask each for help. The first two denied him, saying they had inherited their wealth. The third, who had been blind, said, “I was blind and God restored my sight. Take whatever you want. By God, I won’t hold back anything you take for God’s sake.” The angel said, “Keep your wealth. You have been tested. God is pleased with you and angry with your two companions.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Gratitude is what makes wealth enough. Share what you’ve been given, quickly and without strings. And ask Him for contentment before you ask for more."}]},{"n":90,"tr":"Al-Mani","ar":"المانع","m":"The Preventer","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Mani is the Preventer: the One who holds back and protects. He prevents harm from reaching you, and sometimes He withholds what you want because it isn’t good for you. Behind every “no” from Him is care.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. It is based on verses and hadith that use the same root (“none can withhold what He gives”)."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Whatever mercy God opens for people, none can withhold it. And whatever He withholds, none can release it after Him.” (35:2)\n> “Perhaps you dislike a thing and it is good for you, and perhaps you love a thing and it is bad for you. God knows and you do not know.” (2:216)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"After each prayer he would say: “There is no god but God alone, with no partner. To Him belongs the kingdom and the praise, and He is over all things Capable. O God, none can withhold what You give, and none can give what You withhold, and the wealth of the wealthy cannot benefit him against You.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"On a journey, the Prophet ﷺ was resting alone under a tree when an enemy, Ghawrath, took his sword and raised it above him saying, “Who will protect you from me?” The Prophet ﷺ said, “God.” The sword fell from the man’s hand. The Prophet ﷺ picked it up, asked him the same question, and then let him go without punishment. (Bukhari, Muslim) The One who prevents had prevented the attack."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When a door closes, ask whether you’re being protected. Say the dua after prayer and mean “none can withhold what You give.” And be a *mani* in a small way: stop harm before it starts, even by holding your tongue."}]},{"n":91,"tr":"Ad-Darr","ar":"الضار","m":"The Distresser","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ad-Darr is the One who allows harm to reach whom He wills, such as illness, loss or hardship, always for a reason and always with wisdom. No harm can touch you unless He allows it, and no harm is wasted with Him. It is always understood together with An-Nafi, the Benefactor.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list, and it is traditionally mentioned only together with An-Nafi, never on its own."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “If God touches you with harm, none can remove it but Him. And if He touches you with good, He is over all things Capable.” (6:17)\n> “Say: Who can prevent anything from God if He intends harm for you or intends benefit for you?” (48:11)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He told Ibn Abbas: “Know that if the whole nation gathered to benefit you with something, they could not benefit you except with what God has already written for you. And if they gathered to harm you with something, they could not harm you except with what God has already written against you. The pens have been lifted and the pages have dried.” (Tirmidhi)\nIn his night prayer he would say: “All good is in Your hands, and evil is not attributed to You.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"A doctor who sets a bone or gives a bitter medicine may cause pain, but only to heal. Hardship from God, in the end, is a mercy that we can’t always see yet."},{"h":"Live it","t":"When a hard thing comes, say *inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi raji’un* and look for the lesson. Don’t blame people as if they had power of their own. And hold the pair together: the One who permits hardship is the One who gives relief."}]},{"n":92,"tr":"An-Nafi","ar":"النافع","m":"The Benefactor","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"An-Nafi is the Benefactor: the One who brings benefit, who creates all that is useful, and from whom every good thing comes. Food, medicine, friends, knowledge and ideas are useful only because He made them so.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list and is understood together with Ad-Darr."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “If God touches you with harm, none can remove it but Him. And if He touches you with good, He is over all things Capable.” (6:17)\n> “They worship besides God what can neither benefit them nor harm them.” (10:18)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Ibrahim (Abraham) asked his people, “Do they hear you when you call? Do they benefit you or harm you?” They had no answer except “We found our fathers doing this.” (26:72–74) Later he broke their idols, leaving only the biggest one standing. When the people demanded, “Who did this?”, he said, “Ask the big one, if they can speak.” They lowered their heads and said, “You know that these do not speak.” (21:58–65) Things that cannot speak cannot benefit."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He taught us to ask God for beneficial knowledge: “O God, I ask You for knowledge that is beneficial.” (Ibn Majah) And he said that the best of people are those who are most beneficial to people. (Tabarani, graded hasan)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Ask yourself what is truly beneficial: a habit, a friend, a book, a job. Aim for what benefits you in this world and the next. And be *nafi’* yourself, be useful to people."}]},{"n":93,"tr":"An-Nur","ar":"النور","m":"The Light","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"An-Nur is the Light: He is the source of all light, physical and spiritual. He lights up the heavens and the earth, and He puts light in the hearts of those He guides. Knowledge, truth and guidance are light."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “God is the Light of the heavens and the earth. The example of His light is like a niche in which there is a lamp, the lamp is in a glass, the glass is like a shining star… Light upon light. God guides to His light whom He wills.” (24:35)\n> “On the Day you will see the believing men and women, their light running before them and on their right.” (57:12)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"In his night prayer he would say: “O God, to You belongs all praise. You are the Light of the heavens and the earth and whoever is in them.” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nHe used to pray: “O God, place light in my heart, light in my sight, light in my hearing, light on my right, light on my left, light above me, light below me, light in front of me, light behind me, and make for me light.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Turn on a light in a dark room, and everything that was there all along becomes visible. That is what guidance does for the heart."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Make the Prophet’s ﷺ dua your own, especially on the way to the dawn prayer. Spend time in the places where light grows: the Quran, good company, dhikr. And be a lamp for someone else."}]},{"n":94,"tr":"Al-Hadi","ar":"الهادي","m":"The Guide","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Hadi is the Guide: the One who shows the way to what is true and good, and who puts that guidance in a person’s heart. We can show someone the path. Only He can make a heart walk it."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Guide us to the straight path.” (1:6, said in every unit of prayer)\n> “Your Lord is sufficient as Guide and Helper.” (25:31)\n> “Indeed you do not guide whom you love, but God guides whom He wills.” (28:56)"},{"h":"Story","t":"When Abu Talib, the Prophet’s ﷺ uncle, who had protected him for years, was dying, the Prophet ﷺ sat beside him and said, “Uncle, say ‘There is no god but God.’ It is a word I will testify for you with before God.” Abu Talib did not say it. The verse above was revealed. (Bukhari, Muslim) Even the Messenger could not place guidance in the heart of the one he loved most."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"God says: “O My servants, all of you are astray except those I guide, so ask Me for guidance and I will guide you.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"Ask for guidance every day, as the Fatiha does. Use your own effort and your mind, and keep your heart humble. And be gentle with people who haven’t found their way yet, because you didn’t get there alone either."}]},{"n":95,"tr":"Al-Badi","ar":"البديع","m":"The Originator of the Unprecedented","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Badi is the Originator of the Unprecedented: the One who creates things that never existed before, with no model to copy. Every creation of His is original. He is the source of all beauty and newness.\nNote: the Quran gives it in the form *badi’ as-samawati wal-ard*, “Originator of the heavens and the earth.”"},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “[He is] the Originator of the heavens and the earth. How could He have a child when He has no companion, and He created everything?” (6:101)\n> “The example of Jesus to God is like that of Adam. He created him from dust, then said to him, ‘Be,’ and he was.” (3:59)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Adam had no father or mother, and Jesus had no father. Both were examples of creation with no precedent. The Quran uses them to show that nothing is hard for the Originator. If He wanted, He could create a human in any way."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Whoever introduces something new into this affair of ours (religion) that is not part of it, it will be rejected.” (Bukhari, Muslim) Only God is the Originator of what is new. We are asked to follow what has been given."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Look at the world with fresh eyes. Every sunset is new, as no two skies have ever been the same. And let your own creativity be an act of gratitude: make something good that wasn’t there before."}]},{"n":96,"tr":"Al-Baqi","ar":"الباقي","m":"The Everlasting","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Baqi is the Everlasting: He remains when everything else is gone. He doesn’t age, fade or end. Whatever is done for His sake stays with Him."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Whatever you have will end, and whatever is with God will remain.” (16:96)\n> “Everything will perish except His Face.” (28:88)\n> “Wealth and children are the adornment of this worldly life, but the lasting good deeds are better with your Lord for reward, and better for hope.” (18:46)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “Three things follow a dead person: his family, his wealth and his deeds. Two return, and one remains with him. His family and wealth return; his deeds remain.” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nHe also said: “When a person dies, his deeds come to an end except for three: ongoing charity, knowledge from which others benefit, or a righteous child who prays for him.” (Muslim)"},{"h":"Picture it","t":"Think of what lasts: a house will fall, a bank balance will pass to someone else. A well you dug for people to drink from continues to give long after you are gone."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Invest in what lasts. Teach something. Plant a tree. Dig a well, or support one. And be steady in a small daily deed, because the Prophet ﷺ said the most beloved deeds are the regular ones, even if small. (Bukhari, Muslim)"}]},{"n":97,"tr":"Al-Warith","ar":"الوارث","m":"The Inheritor","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Al-Warith is the Inheritor: when everyone and everything has passed away, all that remains is returned to Him. We think we own things, but we hold them for a short while before they go back to their true owner."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed it is We who give life and cause death, and We are the Inheritors.” (15:23)\n> “Indeed We will inherit the earth and whoever is on it, and to Us they will be returned.” (19:40)\n> “Why do you not spend in the way of God, when to God belongs the inheritance of the heavens and the earth?” (57:10)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Zakariyya (Zechariah) prayed, “My Lord, do not leave me alone, and You are the best of inheritors.” (21:89) He knew that whatever happens to what we leave behind, in the end it is all with God."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"Aisha said that when the Prophet ﷺ died, he left no dinar, no dirham, no sheep and no camel. (Muslim) He said: “We, the prophets, do not leave an inheritance. What we leave behind is charity.” (Bukhari, Muslim) The best man who ever lived took nothing with him."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Write your will. Clear your debts. Think about what you will leave that is worth inheriting: good children, useful knowledge, a good name. And hold your possessions lightly. They are a loan."}]},{"n":98,"tr":"Ar-Rashid","ar":"الرشيد","m":"The Guide to the Right Path","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"Ar-Rashid is the Guide to the Right Path: the One whose guidance leads to a good end, whose decisions are always sound, and who never acts in haste or error. *Rushd* is sound judgment: knowing what is best, and doing it at the right time.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. The Quran uses the same root, as in the cave youths’ dua below."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Our Lord, grant us mercy from Yourself and prepare for us right guidance (*rashad*) in our affair.” (18:10)\n> “And We had certainly given Ibrahim his sound judgment (*rushd*) before.” (21:51)"},{"h":"Story","t":"A group of young believers withdrew from a society that worshipped others besides God, and took shelter in a cave with this dua on their lips. God caused them to sleep for a very long time. When they woke, they found the world had changed around them. (18:9–26) They asked for guidance in an uncertain moment, and He arranged the end."},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He used to pray: “O God, I ask You for steadiness in all matters, and for determination to follow the right path.” (Ahmad, Nasa’i)"},{"h":"Live it","t":"When you have a decision to make, say the cave youths’ dua, ask for *istikhara*, and consult wise people. Don’t rush. And pray to be among those who are *rashidun*, people of sound judgment."}]},{"n":99,"tr":"As-Sabur","ar":"الصبور","m":"The Patient","b":[{"h":"Meaning","t":"As-Sabur is the Patient: He sees every wrong, hears every insult, and still doesn’t rush to punish. He gives people time to repent, and He never acts out of impatience. Unlike human patience, which is a struggle against irritation, God’s patience is perfect.\nNote: this name comes from the traditional list. The Quran says, “God is with the patient” (2:153)."},{"h":"In the Quran","t":"> “Indeed, God is with the patient.” (2:153)\n> “Indeed, the patient will be given their reward in full without account.” (39:10)\n> “If God were to impose blame on people for their wrongdoing, He would not leave on it any creature, but He defers them to a specified term.” (16:61)"},{"h":"From the Prophet ﷺ","t":"He said: “No one is more patient than God over hurtful things they hear. They claim that He has a child, yet He keeps giving them health and provision.” (Bukhari, Muslim)\nHe also said: “Whoever tries to be patient, God will make him patient. And no one has been given a better and wider gift than patience.” (Bukhari, Muslim)"},{"h":"Story","t":"Ayyub (Job) lost his health, wealth and family, and stayed patient for years. God said of him, “Indeed We found him patient, an excellent servant. Indeed he was ever turning back to God.” (38:44) He didn’t pretend he wasn’t in pain. He called on his Lord, and he was answered."},{"h":"Live it","t":"Patience isn’t silence. It is staying steady, doing right, and trusting that relief is coming. When you are tempted to react, remember how patient God has been with you. You have been given more time than you realize. Use it well."}]}];